window.ANTH_INDEX=[{"k": "books", "h": "books/una-cosa-spirituale-vasco-brondi.html", "t": "Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte", "b": "Vasco Brondi", "x": "Central argument In a secular, materialist society, art is the only space left where we can have, even as non-believers, direct contact with magic, with the image that becomes real. Creativity and spirituality aren't opposed: they meet. The book isn't a memoir or a novel. It's an essay on artistic practice as a vertical search: not horizontal expansion (numbers, success, \"does it work?\"), but a descent toward your own centre and a climb back up toward the sky. The thread running through it is the tension between discipline and doing nothing, which Brondi shows to be the same thing: both are \"conquiste dell'inutile\" (\"conquests of the useless\") , practices that have to do with being, not doing. Doing yoga, running, meditating isn't optimising your profile; it's remembering you have a body, lying in wait, getting ready to go deep without burning out. The masters Brondi calls on, from the most imposing (CCCP, Murakami, Kerouac, Grotowski, Jung) to the quietest (his grandmother Cecilia, the woman in the Gospel), aren't there to lend authority. They work as an immune system: knowing that others have already put their hands in the fire gives you the courage to do it again. Objections The book says it wants to \"insegnare come essere poeti senza vivere come poeti\" (\"teach how to be poets without living like poets\") (Bukowski), but it stays closely tied to the experience of an artist who has already made it: the freedom to step out of the market's logic comes after having dealt with it. The list of masters is endless and male (exceptions: Antonia Pozzi, Rosalía). Brondi himself names the risk of turning this crowd into a new theatre, but it stays open. Examples / Notable passages Duende : the sound engineer Gigione after the gig at the centro sociale, \"c'era la gente imbarazzata\" (\"people were embarrassed\") as the highest compliment. Duende is connecting the artistic gesture to your own emotional centre, not the technical virtuosity of scales. The woman in the Gospel (Notizie di me): bleeding for 12 years, she touches Jesus' cloak and is healed. Jesus says: \"La tua fede ti ha salvata\" (\"Your faith has saved you\") . The real miracle isn't the miracle, it's the faith. His debut in 2007-8 had the same structure. The Zen monks with the bell : they sit in a circle and may speak only if their voice trembles. Otherwise, silence. When the voice trembles, something that was deep down is coming out. The immune system of the soul (chapter on Murakami): writing means releasing a toxic substance in the body. To do it for 30 years without dying of it you have to build a strong body. Murakami runs; Brondi does yoga. It's the least romantic thing to say, and that's the point. Hara : in Japanese culture, the point two fingers below the navel where every artistic gesture has to start. Not from ideas in your head. Grotowski let his actors work on art only after 3 hours of exhausting physical training: \"Ok, adesso siete più sinceri.\" (\"OK, now you're more sincere.\") Andy War"}, {"k": "books", "h": "books/women-remembered-bond-taylor.html", "t": "Women Remembered: Jesus' Female Disciples", "b": "Helen K. Bond, Joan E. Taylor", "x": "Central argument Helen K. Bond (Edinburgh) and Joan E. Taylor (King's College London), two historians of Christian origins, argue that Jesus had female disciples who travelled with him, funded the mission, healed, taught and later led churches, and that the all-male picture we carry comes from how they were remembered, not from what they did. The Gospel writers weren't interested in the women, so they show up only in passing, and in Mark only at the cross. The authors read those traces as \"the tip of the iceberg\": in a society that rarely recorded what ordinary women did, every mention counts. From there they go woman by woman (Salome, Joanna and Susanna, Mary Magdalene, the healed women, Martha and Mary, the anointing woman, Mary the mother of Jesus, Tabitha, Prisca, Phoebe and Junia), using the Gospels together with non-canonical texts, art, inscriptions and archaeology. The second half of the argument is about loss: as the movement had to look respectable to Roman society, and even more after Constantine, the women were edited down, veiled and shrunk, until the memory of the first sisters became the exception. Objections The authors ask for \"a good dose of informed imagination\", and they use a lot of it. Men and women sent out in pairs, Joanna healing and baptising other women, Junia being Joanna under a Latin name: each step is plausible, but the chapters pile one plausible step on another, and by the end conjecture reads like a result. Their rule that a text telling women to be silent proves women were speaking is a good tool, but used everywhere it can't lose: if women appear it's the tip of the iceberg, if they don't they were erased. Later and non-canonical material (Gospel of Thomas, Pistis Sophia, Epistula Apostolorum, Hippolytus) gets real weight as \"memory\". The book says how late each one is, but a reader has to keep track of what is first-century evidence and what is third-century legend. The book is openly polemical (\"certain church groups have a vested interest\") and ends on a question (\"where will we go from here?\") without engaging the theological arguments on the other side. It's a historical case, and it leaves the church questions open on purpose. Examples / Notable passages The Last Supper. The book opens with Leonardo's painting , cited as the image that fixed the all-male table in our heads; whoever served the food \"has clearly departed\". Their point is that it shapes boardrooms and cabinets too, not only churches. Romans 16. Of the twenty-nine people Paul greets in Rome, eleven are women. The letter itself was carried by Phoebe , deacon and benefactor, who probably read it out and explained it. A family with no fathers. When his mother and brothers come to take him home, Jesus says anyone who does God's will is my brother and sister and mother (Mark 3:35). Sisters and mothers are in the new family; fathers are missing, because the only father left is God. Women revealed at the cross. In Mark 15:40-41 we learn, only as Jes"}, {"k": "books", "h": "books/the-case-for-christ-lee-strobel.html", "t": "The Case for Christ", "b": "Lee Strobel", "x": "The case, objection by objection A table built chapter by chapter: on the left the skeptical/atheist objection the chapter deals with, on the right the evidence and arguments the book actually presents. Nothing below is made up: every quote and figure can be traced to the text. Objection (the atheist's doubt) Chapter · interviewee Evidence and arguments (from the book) The gospels are anonymous and late: they weren't written by eyewitnesses, so their \"biography of Jesus\" is unreliable at the source Ch. 1 · Craig Blomberg The authorship tradition going back to Papias and Irenaeus (Mark as Peter's interpreter, Luke as Paul's companion); the creed of 1 Corinthians 15:3-8 dated 2 to 8 years after the crucifixion through the technical rabbinic language of \"received/delivered\"; the Aramaic name \"Cephas\"; Paul's investigative visit to Jerusalem (Galatians 1:18-19); Thomas's final confession (\"My Lord and my God!\") as the peak of a high Christology already present in the earliest texts The gospels contain contradictions that can't be reconciled, and invented or legendary details Ch. 2 · Craig Blomberg The \"eight tests\" of historical reliability (intention, ability, character, consistency, bias, cover-up, corroboration, adverse witness); the centurion's story (Matthew/Luke) as complementary perspectives, not contradictory ones; the Gerasenes/Gadarenes question explained by regional naming; the diverging genealogies reconciled as legal line (Joseph) vs blood line (Mary); the criterion of embarrassment (women as first witnesses, the disciples' failures, Jesus' anguish), details a forger wouldn't have invented We only have copies of copies of copies, full of copying errors: the text we read today doesn't match the original Ch. 3 · Bruce Metzger More than 5,000 Greek manuscripts of the NT against the single manuscript of Tacitus (copied in AD 850) or the nine manuscripts of Josephus (11th-12th century); a gap of about 100-150 years between original and first copy against 700-1,400 years for other ancient texts; the papyrus fragment of John 18 dated AD 100-150 by C. H. Roberts; an estimate of 99.5% textual purity (Geisler/Nix); no church doctrine is at stake in the existing variants The NT canon was manipulated for political reasons: \"alternative\" gospels like Thomas were censored for doctrinal reasons Ch. 3 · Bruce Metzger The Gospel of Thomas, dated around AD 140, has no apostolic authority and contains pantheistic (\"split a piece of wood, and I am there\") and misogynistic elements foreign to the canonical gospels; for Metzger it \"excluded itself\". The three historical criteria (apostolic authority, conformity to the rule of faith, continuous use in the church) were applied with broad agreement already in the first two centuries, not decided at a table by a council There are no independent historical sources outside the Bible that confirm Jesus existed or what he claimed Ch. 4 · Edwin Yamauchi Josephus ( Jewish Antiquities , the reference to \"James, the brot"}, {"k": "books", "h": "books/the-practice-of-the-presence-of-god-lawrence.html", "t": "The Practice of the Presence of God: The Best Rule of a Holy Life", "b": "Brother Lawrence", "x": "About the book Brother Lawrence (Nicolas Herman, c. 1614-1691) was a Carmelite lay brother in Paris who spent most of his life working in the monastery kitchen, and later mending sandals. He didn't write this book. After his death Joseph de Beaufort, vicar general of the archbishop of Paris, put together four conversations he had had with him in 1666-1667, his letters and a few short maxims. Highlights The method p. 13, loc. 164: \"That we need only to recognize God intimately present with us, to address ourselves to Him every moment, that we may beg His assistance for knowing His will in things doubtful, and for rightly performing those which we plainly see He requires of us, offering them to Him before we do them, and giving Him thanks when we have done.\" p. 7, loc. 82: \"That we ought to act with God in the greatest simplicity, speaking to Him frankly and plainly, and imploring His assistance in our affairs, just as they happen.\" p. 12, loc. 152: \"That there needed neither art nor science for going to God, but only a heart resolutely determined to apply itself to nothing but Him, or for His sake, and to love Him only.\" p. 34, loc. 426: \"It is not necessary for being with God to be always at church; we may make an oratory of our heart, wherein to retire from time to time, to converse with Him in meekness, humility, and love.\" Work and set times of prayer p. 14, loc. 178: \"That the most excellent method he had found of going to God, was that of doing our common business without any view of pleasing men, and (as far as we are capable) purely for the love of God.\" p. 14, loc. 183: \"That his prayer was nothing else but a sense of the presence of God, his soul being at that time insensible to everything but Divine love: and that when the appointed times of prayer were past, he found no difference, because he still continued with God.\" p. 36, loc. 447: \"One way to recollect the mind easily in the time of prayer, and preserve it more in tranquillity, is not to let it wander too far at other times.\" Wandering and coming back p. 36, loc. 444: \"If it sometimes wander, and withdraw itself from Him, do not much disquiet yourself for that; trouble and disquiet serve rather to distract the mind, than to recollect it; the will must bring it back in tranquillity.\" p. 17, loc. 221: \"When he had finished, he examined himself how he had discharged his duty; if he found well, he returned thanks to God; if otherwise, he asked pardon; and without being discouraged, he set his mind right again, and continued his exercise of the presence of God, as if he had never deviated from it.\" How the habit grows p. 7, loc. 73: \"That in order to form a habit of conversing with God continually, and referring all we do to Him; we must at first apply to Him with some diligence: but that after a little care we should find His love inwardly excite us to it without any difficulty.\" p. 21, loc. 276: \"For the first ten years I suffered much: the apprehension that I was not devoted to God,"}, {"k": "films", "h": "films/elementary.html", "t": "Elementary", "b": "Robert Doherty", "x": "What it's about Modern-day adaptation of Sherlock Holmes set in New York City: Holmes (Jonny Lee Miller), recovering addict, works as a consultant for the NYPD alongside Joan Watson (Lucy Liu), initially hired as his sober companion. CBS procedural, 24 episodes per season, a new case most weeks. Free notes From OSV Field Notes #36 (The OSVerse, 29 August 2026 · https://open.substack.com/pub/infiniteloops/p/osv-field-notes-36 ): a reader (\"Liberty\") on why the series works even though it arrived \"at the wrong time\" to be taken seriously (between Breaking Bad, Mad Men, Game of Thrones, and the glossier BBC Sherlock): The weekly cases and murders are the pretext, not the reason the series holds up. It works because of the casting (Jonny Lee Miller as Holmes, Lucy Liu as Watson) and because of a choice that's never obvious: Sherlock and Joan don't become a romantic couple. They're allowed to become partners (after a rough start), best friends, and in the end almost family. Precisely because it isn't prestige TV and has hundreds of episodes, the two characters spend \"hundreds of hours\" together on screen, and you really see the relationship grow. The other thing that sets this Sherlock apart: he has to work to be Sherlock. When he isn't solving a case, he trains: he reads, picks locks, studies insects or corpses, runs chemistry experiments, practises martial arts. Compared with Cumberbatch's Sherlock (almost supernatural deductions, little visible work) and Robert Downey Jr.'s (more action hero than detective), here genius is the visible result of a method, not a magic gift."}, {"k": "films", "h": "films/la-strada.html", "t": "La Strada", "b": "Federico Fellini", "x": "What it's about Italy, the 1950s. Gelsomina's mother (Gelsomina is a young woman with a simple, almost childlike mind) sells her for 10,000 lire to Zampanò, a brutal travelling showman who makes his living with a strongman act: he breaks an iron chain wrapped around his chest by swelling his muscles. Gelsomina is the second girl he has bought this way: the first, her sister Rosa, died in circumstances the film never explains. With Zampanò. Gelsomina learns to play the bass drum, to blow the trumpet, to do little clown routines to warm up the crowd before his act. Zampanò uses her, beats her, leaves her at night to sleep with other women, then comes back as if nothing had happened. She stays, not for lack of will, but out of an instinctive loyalty she can't explain even to herself. The circus and Il Matto. When they join a small circus, Gelsomina meets Il Matto (\"the Fool\"): a tightrope walker, clown and jester who sees something in her that Zampanò can't see. The two men hate each other more deeply than an ordinary quarrel: Il Matto sees through Zampanò, and that is unforgivable. The pebble scene. Gelsomina is in complete despair. She tells Il Matto she's no use to anyone and is tired of living. He picks up an ordinary stone and points out that even that stone is good for something. He doesn't know what (if he knew, he'd be the Eternal Father), but it must be, because if a stone is useless then everything is useless, even the stars. \"E anche tu, con la testa di carciofo, servi a qualcosa.\" (\"And you too, with your artichoke head, are good for something.\") Gelsomina takes the stone. She decides to stay with Zampanò, because if she goes, who will stay with him? This scene is the structural and philosophical hinge of the film. The decision Gelsomina makes here sets up everything that follows. The death of Il Matto. Zampanò's anger at Il Matto explodes in a fight on a deserted road. He beats him, and this time Il Matto dies under the blows. It isn't clear whether Zampanò meant to kill him: it's his ordinary excess that, for once, has final consequences. He hides the body, gets back in the van and drives off. Gelsomina's collapse. Il Matto's death breaks Gelsomina. She can't come out of it: she stays in a dissociated state and only repeats the sad trumpet melody she and Il Matto shared. Zampanò can't face her, not because he doesn't care, but because he has no tools to do it. One night he leaves her asleep by the side of a road. The death offscreen. Years later. Zampanò is still on the road, still doing his act. He meets a woman hanging out the washing: the strange girl who used to live there, always sad, always playing a tune, has died. Someone had found her and looked after her, and she died like that, in silence. The tune the woman whistles tells him who it was: Gelsomina. The ending on the beach. The news hits Zampanò like a delayed explosion. That night, after getting drunk and picking a fight in a bar, declaring \"I don't need anybody\", he drags "}, {"k": "films", "h": "films/the-case-for-christ.html", "t": "The Case for Christ", "b": "Jon Gunn", "x": "What it's about Based on Lee Strobel's book of the same name (1998). In 1980 Lee Strobel, an investigative journalist and declared atheist, uses his skills as a journalist and legal reporter to try to take apart his wife Leslie's new Christian faith, which opens a rift in their marriage. After almost two years investigating the historical evidence for the resurrection, he finds evidence he didn't expect, and with it a new faith. See the book note"}, {"k": "films", "h": "films/the-madison.html", "t": "The Madison", "b": "Taylor Sheridan", "x": "What it's about The story of a New York family unfolds in the wake of a tragedy, as they come to terms with their grief. Preston Clyburn suddenly dies with his brother during a vacation in rural Montana, leaving his family exploring the bonds between people in the midst of deep sorrow. First reaction The beautiful landscapes and the truth of feelings. It all seemed very real and relatable, unlike many other films and tv series that usually display a fake version of life. What it triggered Grief is a topic which for me is a medium in this series, a moment in which everything comes to surface gifting a huge opportunity: dealing with problems. You can decide to bury them again, or face them. All characters in the series have to face life changing decisions: Stacy will go back to NY or move to Montana? Abigail will finally own her life, try to explore the future with Van? Paige, unemployed, in the middle of a nervous breakdown, how will she deal with it? Free notes Stunning performances, especially from Michelle Pfeiffer (Stacy), Kurt Russell (Preston) and Beau Garrett (Abigail)."}, {"k": "films", "h": "films/vita-semplice.html", "t": "Perfect Days", "b": "Wim Wenders", "x": "What it's about The protagonist of Perfect Days lives a humble, repetitive but attentive life. He doesn't want to be seen. He doesn't compete. And he doesn't seem any less alive for it. First reaction Better mediocre in other people's eyes, but at peace with myself. Not as resignation, but as a deliberate choice to step off the stage. What it triggered Perfection, excellence, grandeur have become default expectations. Contemporary life is performance: a stage where you keep proving your worth, your productivity, your originality. In that system, simplicity looks like failure. Perfect Days shows an alternative. Not romantic or anti-modern, but concrete: an ordinary life lived with attention. Grandeur isn't the only possible form of a good life. Normality, underrated and often mocked, holds a freedom that the race for \"more\" can't offer. Every day, whatever happens, I tell myself: \"better mediocre for everyone else, but at peace with me\". It isn't always easy to keep in mind, because the pressure to prove yourself is everywhere. But there's a real freedom in not having to be extraordinary. A freedom I found in that normality, so underrated and so feared."}, {"k": "podcasts", "h": "podcasts/andrew-garfield-free-myself-into-my-ordinariness.html", "t": "How Jesuit spirituality changed Andrew Garfield's life", "b": "The Spiritual Life (America Media)", "x": "Capture What the episode is about Andrew Garfield talks with James Martin, S.J., who was his spiritual director for seven or eight months while he prepared to play a Jesuit missionary in Martin Scorsese's Silence (2016). Ten years later he talks about his new film The Uprising , acting as a spiritual practice, his mother and her death, what the Spiritual Exercises gave him (above all Jesus's hidden years and the freedom of being ordinary), how he does the examen every night, a dream he had when his mother died, and how not to take your pain out on others. The conversation often turns into a spiritual direction session, as both of them say. After the interview Martin and producer Maggie Van Dorn talk about God speaking through dreams. The episode is 56:47 long; the interview runs from about 4:20 to 51:20. Opening line Played before the intro: \"With social media and with just a kind of the mask of the persona that we give to the world, it's a prison that we're all, I feel, in. And it's a prison that I long to free myself of. Free myself into my ordinariness.\" The full passage comes at 24:28. 0:06 Introduction: how Garfield \"trained as a Jesuit\" Van Dorn's introduction: Golden Globe, Tony, BAFTA, two Oscar nominations; films include The Amazing Spider-Man , Hacksaw Ridge , Tick, Tick... Boom! , We Live in Time . To prepare for Silence he spent the better part of a year in spiritual direction with Martin, doing the Spiritual Exercises of St. Ignatius of Loyola. 1:32 Martin: Silence , from Shūsaku Endō's novel, is about 17th-century Portuguese missionaries in Japan. Garfield and Adam Driver both had to prepare; Garfield chose to do the Exercises: an extended retreat meditating on the life of Christ, done either in 30 days at a retreat house or, as most people do today, as the \"19th annotation\" retreat in daily life over months. They did the whole Exercises in seven or eight months, and Martin says Garfield \"gave himself generously\" and had real experiences of Christ. 2:23 , 2:42 Van Dorn: the Exercises are for anyone, you don't have to be a Jesuit or an actor. You can tell from the way he accesses his depths that he did them. 3:03 The Uprising : \"these are not God's laws\" He sees it as a very spiritual film. It's set during the Peasants' Revolt of 1381 in England, right after the Black Death, which had killed half of Europe. An oligarch class around the young king Richard II (13 or 14) was misusing the recovery funds and hoarding wealth and status, and common people felt their souls squeezed to the point of losing their dignity. 5:28 , 6:10 Enough people rose up to say \"the world is out of joint. These are not God's laws that we are being subjected to. These are man's laws. Because God is more just than this. God wants us to not only survive but to thrive.\" At the time the king was seen as the literal vessel of God's word and law. 6:51 , 7:14 For him it's a story of regular people finding a deeper, more personal relationship with God, and of people ga"}, {"k": "podcasts", "h": "podcasts/brene-brown-jesus-and-vulnerable-spirituality.html", "t": "Brené Brown on Jesus and vulnerable spirituality", "b": "The Spiritual Life (America Media)", "x": "Capture What the episode is about Last episode of the first season of The Spiritual Life , the podcast of the Jesuit magazine America . James Martin, S.J. talks with Brené Brown about her faith: how she became Catholic at seven, why she left the church and later became Episcopalian again, what she means by being a \"mystical Catholic\", how she prays (the examen, her own versions of the Our Father and the Hail Mary), why Jesus is \"tricky\" for her, and how her finding on armor and courage leads her straight to the cross. It ends with the pickleball injury behind her book Strong Ground and her answer on how to return to faith after a long absence. After the interview Martin and his producer Maggie Van Dorn talk about false images of God and explain the examen. The episode is 1:00:36 long; the interview runs from 4:38 to about 51:30. Before the interview: Martin on coming back to faith The listener question (from Nick): how can one return to an active practice of faith after a long absence? Martin's first point: any desire to return to faith, practice or church comes from God. \"How else would God call us other than by awakening the desire in us?\" Taking that seriously makes it less confusing, because you can trust that God is calling you. 2:06 It's natural to come back differently than when you left. Some people have to distance themselves from the faith of their childhood in order to \"reappropriate it in an adult way\"; it's fine if you don't see God or the church the way you did when you were younger. 2:30 Last advice: find a welcoming parish or faith community. 3:00 Raised by a wild pack of Jesuits Baptized in the Episcopal Church in San Antonio, moved to New Orleans at two or three, which she calls her real spiritual formation. She went to Holy Name of Jesus Catholic School. Her line: \"I was raised by a wild pack of Jesuits.\" 5:30 Her house became a way station for Jesuit liberation theologians travelling back from Central America; her mother and her friends were involved in the parish and the school. What she remembers: \"a lot of good New Orleans food and a lot of cocktails and a lot of debate\", with a very conservative father. Martin: \"three things Jesuits like\". 5:59 , 7:05 In second grade (she calls it \"a questionable Catholic experience\") a bishop pulled her out of class and went through the Nicene Creed with her line by line, asking questions. Then he gave her an envelope for her parents: from that point on she was Catholic, not Episcopalian, and they needed to get on board. Her parents took conversion classes. She was seven, and she loved it. 7:36 , 8:36 That's where her lifelong internal conflict with the church started. She loved God, the back and forth of the liturgy, Mass (in Latin once a month); she didn't like rules used in punitive ways. By fifth grade she understood that the discrepancies had nothing to do with God, and they mirrored the ones she saw in her parents: \"You love me but why are you behaving like this? God is love but why"}, {"k": "podcasts", "h": "podcasts/brene-brown-the-opposite-of-courage-is-armor.html", "t": "Brené Brown: The Algorithms Have Forced Us Into A Hidden Epidemic, This Is The Only Way Out!", "b": "The Diary Of A CEO", "x": "Capture What the episode is about Brené Brown, guest on Steven Bartlett's show to talk about her book Strong Ground , goes from her childhood in Texas to power, algorithms, belonging, vulnerability and trust. The thread that holds it together is a finding from 25 years of research: what stops us from being brave isn't fear, it's the armor we put on to protect ourselves when we're afraid. The episode is 1:50:41 long; the last part is about her marriage and her mother's death. Childhood and the two emotional buckets Fifth-generation Texan, parents who did what they could with what they knew, a lot of screaming at home. The allowed emotions were \"pissed off or okay\": sadness and vulnerability were weakness. As the oldest she was the protector of her siblings, and a therapist later called it hypervigilance. 3:34 Six months into her marriage she went to a therapist wanting out. The therapist told her that Steve liked her much more than she liked herself, and that being seen and loved like that must be unbearable when you don't love yourself. She wanted to fire the therapist; it was true. 9:36 If you only have two buckets, every emotion ends up in one of them: when she is scared, grieving, anxious or disappointed, she gets angry. Atlas of the Heart lists 87 emotions because \"the limits of our language are the limits of our world\". 10:50 In their research the average person can name and recognise three emotions: happy, sad, pissed off. 11:06 Power over and fear Citing Jung (the auto-captions say \"Unions\"): before every great progression there is a regression. The way a government uses power tells you what it is afraid of. 15:31 Four kinds of power: power over, power with, power to, power within. Whoever uses power over thinks power is finite, like a pizza: if I give you a slice, I have less. The other three assume it grows when shared. 17:55 Power over is hard to keep because we aren't wired to stay afraid for long: people go numb or get used to it. So whoever leads with fear has to stage \"periodic acts of cruelty\" to put people back into fear. Her example is the masked agents taking immigrants off the street in the US, with children holding on to their parents' legs. 20:47 , 21:44 Permeable boundaries and algorithms From systems theory: a healthy system keeps its boundaries permeable, so feedback flows in and out. When the world gets too complex we stop wanting feedback and close up. The system atrophies and becomes self-referencing: are we right? we're right. 23:41 , 25:40 Bartlett on algorithms: an algorithm that showed you what you don't already believe would be uncomfortable and nobody would use it. She answers \"but great for democracy\". Her summary of where we are: emotionally dysregulated, distrustful of each other, not trusting ourselves, disconnected. She still won't give up on people: \"we are more good than greedy\". 29:22 She proposed \"cognitive sovereignty\" (taking back control of what we read and how we think) to Trevor Noah, who objected: w"}, {"k": "podcasts", "h": "podcasts/dallas-jenkins-plausibilita-e-verita-nel-chosen.html", "t": "I Don't Care About the Reaction - The Chosen Creator Dallas Jenkins", "b": "Within Reason", "x": "Capture What the episode is about Alex O'Connor (philosopher, popular writer, agnostic, known for his channel \"Within Reason\") interviews Dallas Jenkins, creator and director of The Chosen , on the set of the series. The conversation is a long exercise in transparency about the craft of adapting the gospels: where the biblical text ends and dramatic invention begins, what criteria a believing storyteller uses to decide what is \"plausible\" to put in Jesus' mouth, and how actors of different faiths (Jonathan Roumie, a Catholic, who plays Jesus) react to choices that touch their own theology. O'Connor also brings his criteria as a skeptical historian (multiple attestation, the criterion of embarrassment) to weigh which gospel episodes are more or less historically solid. Books and films mentioned in the episode Forrest Gump (film, 1994): used by O'Connor as an analogy for how a viewer holds together a real historical figure with invented characters and events: we know Gump's meeting with JFK never happened, but we know JFK existed. 1:49:56 Titanic (film, 1997): cited by Jenkins as an example of the same narrative device he uses. The opening with the \"modern\" explanation of how the ship sank isn't boring: it frees viewers from having to ask \"how does this work\" so they can sink into the story. The extra-biblical exposition in The Chosen does the same job. 16:54 The West Wing (TV series): Jenkins's stated model for the structure of The Chosen . The President (Jesus) doesn't appear until the last minutes of episode 1; it's an ensemble show that tells the story of power through the people around it. 22:15 Star Wars : mentioned twice as a joke. Once as an example of an implausible motive (Leia handing Luke over to Darth Sidious for money, as an absurd parallel to Judas's supposed motive) 1:25:44 , and once as a yardstick for the entertainment value of The Chosen (\"more interesting than Star Wars\", says O'Connor). 1:59:17 Jesus (film, 1979, a verse-by-verse rendering of the Gospel of Luke): cited by Jenkins as the opposite of what he does, the literal biblical re-enactment, which he finds \"quite emotionally distant\" because it lacks the exposition and story arcs that make a character work on screen. 18:43 Women Remembered: Jesus' Female Disciples ( Women Remembered: Jesus' Female Disciples ), Helen Bond and Joan Taylor (2022): cited by O'Connor. An academic study (Bond teaches at Edinburgh) of why the gospels give women so little narrative space while mentioning them at key moments (funding the ministry, the empty tomb). 1:38:38 Christ before the High Priest (painting, National Gallery, London): O'Connor doesn't name the painter, but it's Gerrit van Honthorst's Christ before the High Priest , c. 1617. He cites it as a possible rival to The Chosen : a single painted look that conveys what the series tells in hours of TV. 1:53:14 The homicide detective's argument (\"97 pieces of the puzzle\") : Jenkins credits it to a detective and apologist whose first name "}, {"k": "podcasts", "h": "podcasts/john-lennox-ai-coscienza-e-speranza-cristiana.html", "t": "No.1 Christianity Expert: If You DON'T Believe In a God You NEED to Hear This!", "b": "The Diary of a CEO", "x": "Capture What the episode is about Steven Bartlett interviews John Lennox, an Oxford mathematician (70+ peer-reviewed papers) and Christian apologist, 82 at the time of recording. The conversation starts from AI (AGI, transhumanism, artificial consciousness) and moves to the classic big questions of apologetics: evidence for God, the resurrection, the problem of evil, hell, the religious \"birth lottery\". Bartlett calls himself agnostic, a former Christian who became a \"new atheist\" at 18 (the Dawkins/Harris phase), and he runs the interview openly as someone still searching. Books mentioned in the episode 2084: Artificial Intelligence and the Future of Humanity , John C. Lennox (2020, updated 2nd edition 2024). Lennox's main book on AI: it defines narrow AI vs AGI and goes through the risks (power, surveillance, unemployment, totalitarian control) and the benefits (above all in medicine). 9:27 God, AI, and the End of History: Understanding the Book of Revelation in the Age of Intelligent Machines , John C. Lennox. Not the AI book as such: it's a reading of the book of Revelation in the light of artificial intelligence. 9:49 My Story: A Spiritual and Intellectual Autobiography , John C. Lennox (SPCK, 2025/2026). His most recent book, which Bartlett recommends at the end of the episode; Lennox tells in detail the experiences he considers \"direct divine guidance\". 1:25:16 Homo Deus: A Brief History of Tomorrow , Yuval Noah Harari (transcribed by mistake in the audio as \"Homodos, the man God\"). Quoted for the thesis of \"self-deification\": the 21st century would have two items on the agenda, solving physical death as a technical problem and increasing human happiness through bioengineering, cyborg engineering and mechanical implants, \"making men gods with a small g\". 6:15 Empire of AI: Dreams and Nightmares in Sam Altman's OpenAI , Karen Hao (2025). Quoted for the metaphor \"the pursuit of the machine god\", which Lennox calls a brilliant summary of what is going on. 11:21 The Matter with Things: Our Brains, Our Delusions, and the Unmaking of the World , Iain McGilchrist (in the audio \"Ian McIchrist\"). The theory of the two hemispheres: for about 500 years the West has favoured the left hemisphere (reductionist, narrow focus) at the expense of the right (the big picture, beauty, art, religion); hence the line \"we understand how almost everything works, but we know the meaning of nothing\". 20:07 A book by C.S. Lewis from the 1940s (the title isn't given in the episode; almost certainly The Abolition of Man , 1943). Lewis goes through about 40 religions and philosophies and finds the golden rule in each one, to show it is \"morally hardwired\" into human beings. 1:00:36 Moments and passages that struck me The distinction between narrow AI and AGI, and the image of AGI as \"a PhD in everything\". 10:12 The knife metaphor : AI is like a good knife, it can be used for surgery or for murder. It isn't evil in itself, but the ethical question always arrives after th"}, {"k": "podcasts", "h": "podcasts/pema-chodron-collaborare-con-il-disagio.html", "t": "This Is Why I Find Pema Chödrön So Essential", "b": "The Ezra Klein Show", "x": "Capture What the episode is about Ezra Klein interviews the Buddhist teacher Pema Chödrön, author of Comfortable With Uncertainty and Another Kind of Freedom . The conversation is about how to relate to discomfort, uncertainty and suffering: not by trying to get rid of them, but by learning to stay inside them, physically and without storylines. Moments that struck me The distinction between pain (the direct experience) and suffering (the storylines we lay on top of it): suffering is \"optional\" because it's built from what we tell ourselves. 25:00 Klein's kids in the rain: they collaborate with reality (they play in the puddles), while Klein resists, trying not to get wet, and gets wet anyway. 23:15 Hot and cold boredom (Trungpa): cold boredom is staying with the discomfort of boredom without wanting to run away, and it's a sign of progress in meditation. Chödrön's story with her mother in Mexico. 38:58 The physical gesture as a way in: touching your heart, or the spot where you feel the contraction, works better than any concept. 10:59 Trungpa's gap : between one breath and the next there's a moment of silence. The story of the fan at the retreat that switches off for an instant and reveals the background. 51:42 Quotes \"The only way out is through.\" (Chödrön, quoting a Buddhist expression) 3:12 \"When we resist change, it's called suffering.\" (Klein, quoting Chödrön's writing) 24:50 \"The amount of growth you're capable of is a direct correlate of the amount of discomfort you're willing to tolerate.\" (something someone once told Klein; Chödrön: \"Oh, that's right on.\") 35:35 \"Refraining is very much the method of becoming a dharmic person. It's a quality of not grabbing for entertainment the minute we feel a slight edge of boredom coming on.\" (Klein, reading Chödrön) 43:20 \"Meditation is not a vacation from irritation.\" 48:50 Kobun Chino Roshi on fear: \"I agree. I agree.\" Not fighting it, agreeing with it. 14:00 Suzuki Roshi: \"You are all perfect just as you are. And you could use a little work.\" 56:29 Exploration Central idea The path to growth doesn't go through getting rid of discomfort but through learning to stay in it: physically, without storylines, with some warmth towards what you feel. Resistance is the source of suffering; collaborating with reality is the door to something more alive. Ideas Discomfort is physical before it's conceptual: locating it in the body is the first step. \"Befriending\" discomfort: not getting rid of it but sending it warmth and tenderness, and if you can't, sending warmth to the fact that you can't. Pain vs suffering: pain is unavoidable, suffering is built by the storylines. Meditation as training to interrupt the storylines (not to get rid of them). \"Collaborating with reality\" vs resistance: Trungpa's snow example. Patience as the ability to stay with restless energy without acting on it right away; the opposite of patience is aggression. Technology has dissolved the daily need to sit with boredom, and that ch"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/stout-statistiche-inventate.html", "t": "There are two kinds of statistics, the kind you look up and the kind you make up.", "b": "Rex Stout", "x": "\"There are two kinds of statistics, the kind you look up and the kind you make up.\" Related in anthology You do not change people's minds by defeating them with logic. — Another line from the same newsletter on how beliefs form, and how little facts and logic weigh."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/tutu-linguaggio-crea-realta.html", "t": "Language is very powerful. Language does not just describe reality. Language creates the reality it describes.", "b": "Desmond Tutu", "x": "\"Language is very powerful. Language does not just describe reality. Language creates the reality it describes.\" Related in anthology We do not think and talk about what we see; we see what we are able to think and talk about. — Schein says the same from the other side: the words we have decide what we are able to see. Be impeccable with your word. Don't take anything personally. Don't make assumptions. Always do your best. — If words create reality, being impeccable with your word is a responsibility, not a nicety."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/agostino-speranza-figlie.html", "t": "Hope has two beautiful daughters; their names are Anger and Courage. Anger at the way things are, and Courage to see that they do not remain as they are.", "b": "Saint Augustine (attr.)", "x": "\"Hope has two beautiful daughters; their names are Anger and Courage. Anger at the way things are, and Courage to see that they do not remain as they are.\" Info & reflection: Hope as active tension, not passive waiting but a pair of forces: anger that sees evil for what it is, courage that refuses to leave it unchanged. Beautiful even with the uncertain source. ⚠️ Uncertain attribution: this quote is widely shared online as Augustine's, but it can't be traced to any of his works. Augustinian scholars class it as apocryphal. Probably a modern paraphrase of his thinking on hope."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/andrea-pazienza-rincorsa.html", "t": "Guardarsi indietro neanche per prendere la rincorsa.", "b": "Andrea Pazienza", "x": "Guardarsi indietro neanche per prendere la rincorsa. Don't look back, not even to take a run-up. Info & reflection: Sorrentino quotes it while explaining that he is no longer melancholic, nostalgic or grieving: \"I didn't process it, I just got tired. Processing takes an intelligence I don't have.\" Pazienza's line is the radical version of the same principle: don't look back, not even for one useful second. The run-up is already an excuse to stand still. Pazienza was a cartoonist and illustrator who died at 32; the line weighs differently once you know that."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-aldila-esperienza.html", "t": "Nell'idea di esperienza di queste società è sempre presente un tipo di «aldilà», che accompagna e sostanzia il qui e ora. La nostra società è l'unica che si nega questa facoltà, frutto di una menomazione essenziale, come se avesse deciso di vivere in un quadro dell'adesso impoverito di tutte le influenze, le azioni, il sussurro, le ispirazioni che vengono da altrove.", "b": "anonymous", "x": "Nell'idea di esperienza di queste società è sempre presente un tipo di «aldilà», che accompagna e sostanzia il qui e ora. La nostra società è l'unica che si nega questa facoltà, frutto di una menomazione essenziale, come se avesse deciso di vivere in un quadro dell'adesso impoverito di tutte le influenze, le azioni, il sussurro, le ispirazioni che vengono da altrove. In these societies' idea of experience there is always some kind of \"beyond\", which goes with the here and now and gives it substance. Ours is the only society that denies itself this faculty, the result of an essential mutilation, as if it had decided to live in a frame of the now stripped of all the influences, the actions, the whisper, the inspirations that come from elsewhere. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Introduzione Info & reflection: modernity as the only culture that cut off its own beyond. Not an absence but a chosen mutilation. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — The quote is in Brondi's introduction: in a materialist society that has given up the beyond, art is the last contact with the sacred"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-bussola-orologio.html", "t": "My neighbour once told me to never forget that the compass was invented before the clock because direction is more important than time.", "b": "anonymous", "x": "My neighbour once told me to never forget that the compass was invented before the clock because direction is more important than time. Info & reflection: the compass (where you're going) comes before the clock (how fast). Knowing which direction to move in is more urgent than knowing when."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-letteratura-abisso.html", "t": "c'è invece una letteratura che nasce per introdurre al cuore piú profondo della realtà, lí dove le cose trovano il loro fondamento, la loro piú nascosta affidabilità. […] ci porta dentro l'abisso delle cose, in una maniera cosí sorprendente e indelebile che non si può piú essere le stesse persone di prima. […] riuscendo a diseppellire il mistero delle cose.", "b": "anonymous", "x": "c'è invece una letteratura che nasce per introdurre al cuore piú profondo della realtà, lí dove le cose trovano il loro fondamento, la loro piú nascosta affidabilità. […] ci porta dentro l'abisso delle cose, in una maniera cosí sorprendente e indelebile che non si può piú essere le stesse persone di prima. […] riuscendo a diseppellire il mistero delle cose. there is, instead, a literature born to lead us into the deepest heart of reality, where things find their foundation, their most hidden reliability. […] it takes us into the abyss of things, in a way so surprising and indelible that we can no longer be the same people we were before. […] managing to unearth the mystery of things. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Introduzione Info & reflection: literature as unearthing the mystery: not explanation but descent. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — quote from the book's introduction, where Brondi describes literature that goes down into the abyss of things and unearths the mystery"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-nulla-si-guadagna-senza-perdere.html", "t": "Nothing can be gained without losing, / Even the heavens demand death.", "b": "anonymous", "x": "Nothing can be gained without losing, Even the heavens demand death. Original source unknown. Circulating on Instagram/TikTok since 2024, attributed to Seneca, De Providentia. The attribution is false: no passage of the Latin text matches this wording. Info & reflection: the idea of loss as the condition for gain. Not an exception but a structural law, reaching as far as the cosmos itself."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-odiati-dagli-idioti.html", "t": "Being hated by idiots is the price you pay for not being one of them.", "b": "anonymous", "x": "Being hated by idiots is the price you pay for not being one of them. Related in anthology The eyes of others our prisons; their thoughts our cages. — other people's gaze as a prison that integrity walks out of"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-riservatezza-privacy.html", "t": "private people have fun too, we just don't care if you know", "b": "", "x": "private people have fun too, we just don't care if you know"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anonimo-tristezza-oscurita.html", "t": "I can give you my loneliness, / my darkness, the hunger of my heart; / I am trying to bribe you with uncertainty, / with danger, with defeat.", "b": "Jorge Luis Borges", "x": "I can give you my loneliness, my darkness, the hunger of my heart; I am trying to bribe you with uncertainty, with danger, with defeat. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Notizie di me Info & reflection: offering your own darkness as a gift. Not the light, not the strength. Attribution: Jorge Luis Borges, \"Two English Poems\" II (1934), written in English. Brondi quotes it in Italian without the author's name, with \"sadness\" where Borges wrote \"loneliness\". Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes it in the chapter Notizie di me, the one about the woman in the Gospel: offering sadness and uncertainty instead of strength"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/anthony-bloom-sinner-saint.html", "t": "God can save the sinner you are, but not the saint you pretend to be.", "b": "Anthony Bloom", "x": "\"God can save the sinner you are, but not the saint you pretend to be.\" Anthony Bloom (Metropolitan Anthony of Sourozh, 1914–2003) — Russian Orthodox bishop, spiritual writer. Context: God cannot work with a facade. If you wear a mask of perfection (the \"saint you pretend to be\"), you leave no room for grace to heal the actual areas of your life that need change. Related in anthology Il termine greco utilizzato nei Vangeli per designare il peccato deriva dal verbo amartano , che significa letteralmente «fallire il bersaglio» ed è un termine tecnico usato dagli arcieri. […] Gli impulsi passionali, la freccia, sono cosa sacra e buona, il problema è dove miri, quale è il bersaglio a cui tendi, verso che direzione hai scoccato la freccia. — same structure: we are not condemned by sin (or by the mask) but by the wrong direction — the arrow is sacred, the problem is where you aim; Bloom takes it further: God works with the real sinner, not with the saint on the surface"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/aristotele-mente-educata.html", "t": "It is the mark of an educated mind to be able to entertain a thought without accepting it.", "b": "Aristotle (attr., apocryphal)", "x": "\"It is the mark of an educated mind to be able to entertain a thought without accepting it.\" Info & reflection: The idea itself is a precise epistemic skill: being able to hold a thought, examine it and let it go without feeling threatened and without having to adopt it. Separating understanding from acceptance. It is also a description of intellectual maturity as a stance, not as content. ⚠️ Apocryphal attribution: this quote circulates as Aristotle's but it does not appear in any of his works. The first documented occurrence is in Lowell Bennion's Religion and the Pursuit of Truth (1959), where it is presented as inspired by Aristotle's thought, not quoted directly. The likely root is the real Aristotle of the Nicomachean Ethics , I.3 (W. D. Ross translation): \"It is the mark of an educated man to look for precision in each class of things just so far as the nature of the subject admits.\" The authentic idea is about epistemic precision, which is different from the tolerance of other people's thoughts in the apocryphal quote, though related. Classification: apocryphal."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/arminio-zone-lontane.html", "t": "«La prima volta non fu quando ci spogliammo ma qualche giorno prima, mentre parlavi sotto un albero. / Sentivo zone lontane del mio corpo che tornavano a casa.»", "b": "Franco Arminio", "x": "«La prima volta non fu quando ci spogliammo ma qualche giorno prima, mentre parlavi sotto un albero. Sentivo zone lontane del mio corpo che tornavano a casa.» The first time was not when we undressed but a few days before, while you were talking under a tree. I felt distant parts of my body coming back home."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/basquiat-spazio-tempo.html", "t": "Art is how we decorate space, music is how we decorate time.", "b": "Jean-Michel Basquiat", "x": "Art is how we decorate space, music is how we decorate time. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Quando non succede niente Info & reflection: the space/time distinction as a key to understanding the two art forms. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Basquiat in the chapter Quando non succede niente: art and music as ways of decorating space and time"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/battiato-tramonto-parchetto.html", "t": "La cantante Alice racconta che Franco Battiato, quando registrava con lei nello studio di Milano, voleva sempre fare una pausa nel tardo pomeriggio per vedere il tramonto in un parchetto lí vicino, come faceva nel suo paese in Sicilia, sulle pendici dell'Etna. Il sole non lo vedevano veramente tramontare perché c'erano i palazzi a coprire l'orizzonte, era soltanto la luce a cambiare, ma quel riconnettersi per un attimo con i cicli del giorno e della notte era diventato un momento importante.", "b": "Franco Battiato", "x": "La cantante Alice racconta che Franco Battiato, quando registrava con lei nello studio di Milano, voleva sempre fare una pausa nel tardo pomeriggio per vedere il tramonto in un parchetto lí vicino, come faceva nel suo paese in Sicilia, sulle pendici dell'Etna. Il sole non lo vedevano veramente tramontare perché c'erano i palazzi a coprire l'orizzonte, era soltanto la luce a cambiare, ma quel riconnettersi per un attimo con i cicli del giorno e della notte era diventato un momento importante. The singer Alice tells how Franco Battiato, when he was recording with her in the studio in Milan, always wanted to take a break in the late afternoon to watch the sunset in a small park nearby, as he used to do in his home town in Sicily, on the slopes of Etna. They never really saw the sun go down, because buildings blocked the horizon; only the light changed. But that moment of reconnecting with the cycles of day and night had become important. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Una noia necessaria Info & reflection: the ritual counts even when the gesture is imperfect. The changing light is enough, you don't need the real sunset. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Battiato going out to watch the light change in a small park in Milan, Brondi's example of the laws of the universe inside the city"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/battiato-tutti-assieme.html", "t": "Battiato raccontava di finire ogni meditazione quotidiana con una formula semplice e di sua invenzione: «Per tutti gli esseri, tutti assieme».", "b": "Franco Battiato", "x": "Battiato raccontava di finire ogni meditazione quotidiana con una formula semplice e di sua invenzione: «Per tutti gli esseri, tutti assieme». Battiato used to say he ended every daily meditation with a simple formula of his own: \"For all beings, all together\". via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Tutti assieme Info & reflection: how simple the formula is. A few words to undo the illusion of separation. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the chapter Tutti assieme, Battiato's formula goes with the idea from Jung that Brondi takes up: the biggest illusion is feeling separate"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/benjamin-consapevolezza-senza-spavento.html", "t": "To be happy is to be able to become aware of oneself without fright.", "b": "Walter Benjamin", "x": "\"To be happy is to be able to become aware of oneself without fright.\" Info & reflection: Here happiness isn't a mood but an ability: looking inside yourself without backing away from what you find. It turns around the common idea that self-awareness is tiring or painful. For Benjamin it's the opposite: what stops happiness is the fear of your own inner gaze, not introspection itself."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/blackshear-vigilanti.html", "t": "Vigilantes nella notte (Watchers in the Night)", "b": "Thomas Blackshear II", "x": "God protects us without our knowing it, even from dangers we can't even imagine"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/bohr-ferro-di-cavallo.html", "t": "Niels Bohr had a horseshoe hanging on the door of his study. A visitor asked him if he really believed it brought luck. Bohr replied: \"Of course not... but I am told it works even if you don't believe in it.", "b": "Niels Bohr", "x": "Niels Bohr had a horseshoe hanging on the door of his study. A visitor asked him if he really believed it brought luck. Bohr replied: \"Of course not... but I am told it works even if you don't believe in it.\" via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Ritualità Info & reflection: the rite works regardless of faith. It doesn't need belief to be effective. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Bohr's horseshoe in the Ritualità chapter: the rite works even if you don't believe in it, like daily discipline for Brondi"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/bonolis-peso-soggettivo.html", "t": "Le cose hanno tutte un peso oggettivo. Se tu ci metti il tuo, il peso aumenta, diventa insostenibile. Ma quello è un peso soggettivo che tu ti dai. Dobbiamo vivere con la consapevolezza che nella vita ci possono accadere cose bellissime e anche cose molto spiacevoli. È fatta così per tutti. Quando siete felici, fateci caso. Come se le cose belle ci fossero dovute dall'esistenza — non è così. È una grazia che ti capita. Non ci accorgiamo solo delle cose che vanno male, come se ci arrivassero disgrazie da un destino cinico e baro. La realtà è che la vita è un insieme di tutto: cose belle, cose brutte, luce e ombre. Se riesci a vedere solo le ombre, è un peccato e un grande spreco.", "b": "Paolo Bonolis", "x": "Le cose hanno tutte un peso oggettivo. Se tu ci metti il tuo, il peso aumenta, diventa insostenibile. Ma quello è un peso soggettivo che tu ti dai. Dobbiamo vivere con la consapevolezza che nella vita ci possono accadere cose bellissime e anche cose molto spiacevoli. È fatta così per tutti. Quando siete felici, fateci caso. Come se le cose belle ci fossero dovute dall'esistenza — non è così. È una grazia che ti capita. Non ci accorgiamo solo delle cose che vanno male, come se ci arrivassero disgrazie da un destino cinico e baro. La realtà è che la vita è un insieme di tutto: cose belle, cose brutte, luce e ombre. Se riesci a vedere solo le ombre, è un peccato e un grande spreco. Things all have an objective weight. If you add your own, the weight grows and becomes unbearable. But that is a subjective weight you give yourself. We have to live knowing that in life very beautiful things can happen to us, and very unpleasant things too. It's like that for everyone. When you are happy, notice it. As if beautiful things were owed to us by existence: they aren't. It's a grace that happens to you. Let's not notice only the things that go wrong, as if misfortunes came to us from a cynical, crooked fate. The truth is that life is a mix of everything: good things, bad things, light and shadows. If you can only see the shadows, it's a pity and a great waste. Info & reflection: Two ideas that hold together. The first: the pain of things has an objective share that can't be removed, but there's also a subjective share we add ourselves, and that's the part that makes it unbearable. The second, even rarer: \"When you are happy, notice it.\" We don't pay attention to happiness because we treat it as owed, but it isn't. It's a grace. Recognising grace is an active act, not a passive one. He also quotes the film Being There (1979): \"Life is a state of mind.\" Related in anthology This Is Why I Find Pema Chödrön So Essential — Pema Chödrön separates pain, the direct experience, from the suffering built by the stories we tell ourselves on top of it"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/bormolini-amartano.html", "t": "Il termine greco utilizzato nei Vangeli per designare il peccato deriva dal verbo *amartano*, che significa letteralmente «fallire il bersaglio» ed è un termine tecnico usato dagli arcieri. […] Gli impulsi passionali, la freccia, sono cosa sacra e buona, il problema è dove miri, quale è il bersaglio a cui tendi, verso che direzione hai scoccato la freccia.", "b": "Guidalberto Bormolini", "x": "Il termine greco utilizzato nei Vangeli per designare il peccato deriva dal verbo amartano , che significa letteralmente «fallire il bersaglio» ed è un termine tecnico usato dagli arcieri. […] Gli impulsi passionali, la freccia, sono cosa sacra e buona, il problema è dove miri, quale è il bersaglio a cui tendi, verso che direzione hai scoccato la freccia. The Greek word used in the Gospels for sin comes from the verb amartano , which literally means \"to miss the target\" and is a technical term used by archers. […] The passionate impulses, the arrow, are sacred and good; the problem is where you aim, what target you are reaching for, in which direction you have shot the arrow. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — L'animale che mi porto dentro Info & reflection: sin is not passion but the wrong direction. The arrow is sacred. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi uses amartano in the chapter L'animale che mi porto dentro: passion is sacred, what matters is the target, like finding a food worthy of our insatiability Addiction is proof that you're capable of intense devotion. You are just serving the wrong god. — \"serving the wrong god\" is amartano in a modern key: same arrow, wrong direction God can save the sinner you are, but not the saint you pretend to be. — Bloom names the opposite trap: a perfect façade is a wrong target too; \"God can save the sinner you are\" is the Christian answer to amartano How Jesuit spirituality changed Andrew Garfield's life — Andrew Garfield: sin as a missed aim in his daily examen Brené Brown on Jesus and vulnerable spirituality — Brené Brown rejects \"sinner\"; reading amartano as a missed aim would give her another way in"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/brondi-spazio-fisico.html", "t": "In uno dei primi libri che ho letto, Thich Nhat Hanh parlava dell'importanza di crearsi in casa una stanza per meditare – diceva «per respirare». Anche solo un angolo, anche solo un cuscino. Ma che fosse un punto preciso, dedicato a questo. Diceva che quello spazio avrebbe alimentato la pratica, e che avrebbe infuso nel resto della casa quell'energia di consapevolezza, quella possibilità di stare con le cose cosí come sono. Era importante creare uno spazio fisico.", "b": "Vasco Brondi", "x": "In uno dei primi libri che ho letto, Thich Nhat Hanh parlava dell'importanza di crearsi in casa una stanza per meditare – diceva «per respirare». Anche solo un angolo, anche solo un cuscino. Ma che fosse un punto preciso, dedicato a questo. Diceva che quello spazio avrebbe alimentato la pratica, e che avrebbe infuso nel resto della casa quell'energia di consapevolezza, quella possibilità di stare con le cose cosí come sono. Era importante creare uno spazio fisico. In one of the first books I read, Thich Nhat Hanh talked about how important it is to make a room for meditation at home; he said \"for breathing\". Even just a corner, even just a cushion. But a precise spot, set aside for this. He said that space would feed the practice, and would fill the rest of the house with that energy of awareness, that chance to be with things as they are. It was important to make a physical space. Info & reflection: the physical space isn't an extra to the practice. It feeds it, and changes the place around it. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi tells of the meditation corner suggested by Thich Nhat Hanh, an example of the daily practices the book rests on"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/buddismo-meditare-un-ora.html", "t": "Un allievo va dal suo maestro di meditazione e gli chiede quanto tempo dovrebbe dedicare alla pratica quotidiana. Il maestro gli risponde che venti minuti al giorno vanno benissimo, tranne quando proprio non ha tempo: in quei giorni dovrebbe meditare per un'ora.", "b": "Buddhist tradition", "x": "Un allievo va dal suo maestro di meditazione e gli chiede quanto tempo dovrebbe dedicare alla pratica quotidiana. Il maestro gli risponde che venti minuti al giorno vanno benissimo, tranne quando proprio non ha tempo: in quei giorni dovrebbe meditare per un'ora. A student goes to his meditation teacher and asks how much time he should give to daily practice. The teacher answers that twenty minutes a day is fine, except when he really has no time: on those days he should meditate for an hour. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Quando non succede niente Info & reflection: the paradox that turns the logic of efficiency upside down: when you have no time is exactly when you need it most. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — the teacher who asks for an hour of meditation on the days with no time, from the chapter Quando non succede niente: the opposite of productive logic"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/buddismo-precipitare-senza-pavimento.html", "t": "The bad news is you're falling through the air, nothing to hang on to, no parachute. The good news is, there's no ground.", "b": "Buddhist tradition", "x": "The bad news is you're falling through the air, nothing to hang on to, no parachute. The good news is, there's no ground. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Ritualità Info & reflection: free fall with no impact: terror and relief in the same image. Attribution: in English the line is credited to Chögyam Trungpa, the Tibetan Buddhist teacher. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — the fall with no floor, from the chapter Ritualità: letting go of the handholds, like the blind faith Brondi puts at the base of creative work This Is Why I Find Pema Chödrön So Essential — Pema Chödrön and Comfortable With Uncertainty: staying in uncertainty and discomfort with the body, without the stories we add to them"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/calvino-granchio.html", "t": "Tra le molte virtù di Chuang-Tzu c'era l'abilità nel disegno. Il re gli chiese il disegno d'un granchio. Chuang-Tzu disse che aveva bisogno di cinque anni di tempo e d'una villa con dodici servitori. Dopo cinque anni il disegno non era ancora cominciato. \"Ho bisogno di altri cinque anni\", disse Chuang-Tzu. Il re glieli accordò. Allo scadere dei dieci anni, Chuang-Tzu prese il pennello e in un istante, con un solo gesto, disegnò un granchio, il più perfetto granchio che si fosse mai visto.", "b": "Italo Calvino", "x": "Tra le molte virtù di Chuang-Tzu c'era l'abilità nel disegno. Il re gli chiese il disegno d'un granchio. Chuang-Tzu disse che aveva bisogno di cinque anni di tempo e d'una villa con dodici servitori. Dopo cinque anni il disegno non era ancora cominciato. \"Ho bisogno di altri cinque anni\", disse Chuang-Tzu. Il re glieli accordò. Allo scadere dei dieci anni, Chuang-Tzu prese il pennello e in un istante, con un solo gesto, disegnò un granchio, il più perfetto granchio che si fosse mai visto. Among Chuang-Tzu's many virtues was his skill at drawing. The king asked him to draw a crab. Chuang-Tzu said he needed five years and a country house with twelve servants. After five years the drawing had not yet been started. \"I need another five years,\" said Chuang-Tzu. The king granted them. At the end of the ten years, Chuang-Tzu took up his brush and in an instant, with a single stroke, drew a crab, the most perfect crab ever seen."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/camus-bisogno-aver-ragione.html", "t": "The need to be right is the sign of a vulgar mind.", "b": "Albert Camus", "x": "\"The need to be right is the sign of a vulgar mind.\" Info & reflection: Camus ties vulgarity not to a lack of culture but to a psychological need: whoever must always be right gives away an insecurity they mistake for strength. It's an implicit criticism of a certain way of arguing, the one that looks for victory instead of truth. Related in anthology You do not change people's minds by defeating them with logic. — The same need to be right explains why logic doesn't change minds. The more we justify our beliefs, the more narrow-minded we become. — Lin Yutang links narrow convictions and lack of humour, the same rigidity as the need to be right. No one wants the truth if it is inconvenient. — the other side: refusing the truth when it's uncomfortable"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/caravaggio-chiamata-di-san-matteo.html", "t": "The Calling of Saint Matthew", "b": "Caravaggio", "x": "Related in anthology Cristo di San Giovanni della Croce — Like Dalí's Crucifixion, it reframes a sacred moment from an unsettling angle."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/casalino-bruciare-le-navi.html", "t": "Il detto \"bruciare le navi\" nasce da una storia vera. / Nel 1519 Hernán Cortés arrivò in Messico con i suoi uomini. La terra era sconosciuta, il rischio enorme, la paura reale. / Molti volevano tornare indietro: il mare era lì, le navi pronte, la via di fuga aperta. / Cortés fece una scelta estrema. / Ordinò di distruggere le navi. Niente ritorno. / Nessun piano B. / Nessuna alternativa rassicurante. / Da quel momento non c'era più la possibilità di scappare. / C'era solo una direzione: avanti. / E proprio perché non esisteva un'altra scelta, trovarono una forza che non sapevano di avere. / \"Bruciare le navi\" significa questo: / tagliare le vie di fuga, smettere di tenere un piede nel passato e decidere, una volta per tutte, chi vuoi diventare.", "b": "Alessia Casalino", "x": "Il detto \"bruciare le navi\" nasce da una storia vera. Nel 1519 Hernán Cortés arrivò in Messico con i suoi uomini. La terra era sconosciuta, il rischio enorme, la paura reale. Molti volevano tornare indietro: il mare era lì, le navi pronte, la via di fuga aperta. Cortés fece una scelta estrema. Ordinò di distruggere le navi. Niente ritorno. Nessun piano B. Nessuna alternativa rassicurante. Da quel momento non c'era più la possibilità di scappare. C'era solo una direzione: avanti. E proprio perché non esisteva un'altra scelta, trovarono una forza che non sapevano di avere. \"Bruciare le navi\" significa questo: tagliare le vie di fuga, smettere di tenere un piede nel passato e decidere, una volta per tutte, chi vuoi diventare. The saying \"burn the ships\" comes from a true story. In 1519 Hernán Cortés landed in Mexico with his men. The land was unknown, the risk huge, the fear real. Many wanted to go back: the sea was there, the ships ready, the escape route open. Cortés made an extreme choice. He ordered the ships destroyed. No going back. No plan B. No reassuring alternative. From that moment there was no way to run. There was only one direction: forward. And precisely because there was no other choice, they found a strength they didn't know they had. That's what \"burning the ships\" means: cutting off the escape routes, no longer keeping one foot in the past, and deciding once and for all who you want to become. Info & reflection: Total commitment as a source of strength. Getting rid of plan B isn't recklessness, it's a deliberate act that frees energy. If you keep one foot in the past you can never push with all your weight."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/cave-duende.html", "t": "Federico García Lorca attempts to shed some light on the eerie and inexplicable sadness that lives at the heart of certain works of art. \"All that has dark sounds has *duende*,\" he says […]. Contemporary rock music seems less inclined to have at its soul, restless and quivering, the sadness that Lorca talks about. […] Bob Dylan has always had it. Leonard Cohen deals specifically with it. It pursues Van Morrison like a black dog […]. My friends The Dirty 3 have it by the bucketload. But, all in all, it would appear that the *duende* is too fragile to survive the compulsive modernity of the music industry.", "b": "Nick Cave", "x": "Federico García Lorca attempts to shed some light on the eerie and inexplicable sadness that lives at the heart of certain works of art. \"All that has dark sounds has duende ,\" he says […]. Contemporary rock music seems less inclined to have at its soul, restless and quivering, the sadness that Lorca talks about. […] Bob Dylan has always had it. Leonard Cohen deals specifically with it. It pursues Van Morrison like a black dog […]. My friends The Dirty 3 have it by the bucketload. But, all in all, it would appear that the duende is too fragile to survive the compulsive modernity of the music industry. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Shock telepatico Info & reflection: duende isn't a technical quality but something too fragile to survive the logic of the industry. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Nick Cave on duende, quoted by Brondi: too fragile for the music industry, like sincerity that turns cringe in the aesthetics of cute"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/cave-felice-di-essere-triste.html", "t": "Twenty years of songwriting have now passed, and still the void gapes wide. Still the inexplicable sadness, the *duende*, the *saudade*, the divine discontent, persists, and perhaps it will continue until I see the face of God himself. But when Moses desired to see the face of God, he was answered that he may not endure it, that no man could see the face of God and live. Well, me, I don't mind. I'm happy to be sad. For the residue cast off in this search, the songs themselves, my crooked brood of sad-eyed children, rally round and in their way protect me, comfort me and keep me alive. They are the companions of the soul that lead it into exile, that sate the overpowering yearning for that which is not of this world.", "b": "Nick Cave", "x": "Twenty years of songwriting have now passed, and still the void gapes wide. Still the inexplicable sadness, the duende , the saudade , the divine discontent, persists, and perhaps it will continue until I see the face of God himself. But when Moses desired to see the face of God, he was answered that he may not endure it, that no man could see the face of God and live. Well, me, I don't mind. I'm happy to be sad. For the residue cast off in this search, the songs themselves, my crooked brood of sad-eyed children, rally round and in their way protect me, comfort me and keep me alive. They are the companions of the soul that lead it into exile, that sate the overpowering yearning for that which is not of this world. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Shock telepatico Info & reflection: the residue of the search for the unreachable: songs as companions of the soul. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the chapter Shock telepatico Brondi quotes Cave on duende: the unexplained sadness that songs don't close but keep alive"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celati-camminare-per-scrivere.html", "t": "Camminare è fondamentale per scrivere perché la fatica che comporta, una volta a casa, garantisce che quello che si andrà a mettere sulla pagina bianca sarà solo lo stretto necessario.", "b": "Gianni Celati", "x": "Camminare è fondamentale per scrivere perché la fatica che comporta, una volta a casa, garantisce che quello che si andrà a mettere sulla pagina bianca sarà solo lo stretto necessario. Walking is essential for writing because the effort it takes guarantees that, once you are home, what you put on the blank page will be only what is strictly necessary. Info & reflection: walking as a creative filter. Physical effort works as a sieve and lets through only what is really worth it."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-bruco-paura-del-bozzolo.html", "t": "L'unica alternativa all'attraversamento è rimanere bruco per sempre, non per scelta ma per paura del bozzolo.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "L'unica alternativa all'attraversamento è rimanere bruco per sempre, non per scelta ma per paura del bozzolo. The only alternative to going through it is to stay a caterpillar forever, not by choice but out of fear of the cocoon. Info & reflection: It makes the real choice visible: it isn't \"going through the effort\" vs \"not making it\", it's going through vs staying still out of fear. The second option isn't neutral, it's a precise, silent cost."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-comportamento-vs-identita.html", "t": "Il cambiamento lavora sul comportamento. La trasformazione lavora sull'identità.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "Il cambiamento lavora sul comportamento. La trasformazione lavora sull'identità. Change works on behaviour. Transformation works on identity. Info & reflection: It separates two very different levels of intervention. Working on behaviour is surface level; working on narrative identity is structural. The difference is in how we tell ourselves who we are: I want to stop doing this vs I am a person who doesn't do this ."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-conformita-autenticita.html", "t": "La conformità è una categoria sociale: serve agli altri per sapere cosa aspettarsi da noi, per classificarci, per collocarci in modo prevedibile all'interno di un ordine che non abbiamo scelto. L'autenticità è una categoria interiore: serve a noi per rimanere in contatto con ciò che sentiamo vero in ogni fase della nostra vita. Le due non sempre coincidono. Anzi, nei momenti di vera trasformazione quasi mai coincidono.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "La conformità è una categoria sociale: serve agli altri per sapere cosa aspettarsi da noi, per classificarci, per collocarci in modo prevedibile all'interno di un ordine che non abbiamo scelto. L'autenticità è una categoria interiore: serve a noi per rimanere in contatto con ciò che sentiamo vero in ogni fase della nostra vita. Le due non sempre coincidono. Anzi, nei momenti di vera trasformazione quasi mai coincidono. Conformity is a social category: it lets others know what to expect from us, classify us, place us predictably inside an order we didn't choose. Authenticity is an inner category: it lets us stay in touch with what we feel is true at every stage of our life. The two don't always match. In fact, in moments of real transformation they almost never do. Info & reflection: It makes the tension in moments of real change legitimate. Looking \"out of line\" to people who knew you before isn't a problem. It's the sign that the transformation is real, not a performance. Related in anthology The eyes of others our prisons; their thoughts our cages. — other people's gaze as a prison: the price of conformity Brené Brown: The Algorithms Have Forced Us Into A Hidden Epidemic, This Is The Only Way Out! — Brené Brown: to fit into a group the first person you betray is yourself, and fitting in is the biggest threat to belonging"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-direzione-non-risposta-fissa.html", "t": "E la direzione, non la risposta fissa, è tutto ciò di cui abbiamo bisogno per camminare.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "E la direzione, non la risposta fissa, è tutto ciò di cui abbiamo bisogno per camminare. And direction, not a fixed answer, is all we need in order to walk. Info & reflection: It takes away the weight of having to know \"who I am\" once and for all. The answer is never complete, but the direction is enough. Every provisional answer is a data point; put together over time, they tell a trajectory."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-il-se-non-si-trova.html", "t": "Il sé non si trova. Si narra. E ogni narrazione è provvisoria, non nel senso di falsa, ma nel senso di aperta al capitolo successivo.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "Il sé non si trova. Si narra. E ogni narrazione è provvisoria, non nel senso di falsa, ma nel senso di aperta al capitolo successivo. The self is not found. It is narrated. And every narration is provisional, not in the sense of false, but in the sense of open to the next chapter. Info & reflection: It solves the problem at the root: the question \"who am I?\" is badly put if it expects a final answer. Identity is not a hidden object to discover but a narrative process, always under construction. Every answer is provisional and open: not false, only incomplete."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-liberta-natura-profonda.html", "t": "La libertà non è la capacità di cambiare le circostanze esterne. È la capacità di rimanere fedeli alla propria natura profonda dentro qualsiasi circostanza.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "La libertà non è la capacità di cambiare le circostanze esterne. È la capacità di rimanere fedeli alla propria natura profonda dentro qualsiasi circostanza. Freedom is not the ability to change external circumstances. It is the ability to stay true to your deepest nature in any circumstance. Info & reflection: Epictetus via Celli: freedom read as inner faithfulness, not as control of your surroundings. Real freedom doesn't depend on what happens outside; it depends on how far you manage to stay yourself inside whatever happens. Related in anthology The eyes of others our prisons; their thoughts our cages. — the opposite of inner freedom: living in the cages of other people's thoughts"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-non-diventi-qualcuno-nuovo.html", "t": "Non diventi qualcuno di nuovo. Diventi più pienamente ciò che sei sempre stato, prima che il mondo cominciasse a chiederti di essere qualcos'altro.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "Non diventi qualcuno di nuovo. Diventi più pienamente ciò che sei sempre stato, prima che il mondo cominciasse a chiederti di essere qualcos'altro. You don't become someone new. You become more fully what you have always been, before the world started asking you to be something else. Info & reflection: Transformation as revelation, not construction: Celli by way of the Upanishads. You don't add something that isn't there; you remove what covers what was always there. One of the most precise ways of putting the idea of authenticity I've read."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-pattern-etichette-terapeutico.html", "t": "I pattern sono dinamici, contestuali, modificabili. Le etichette sembrano fisse. La differenza non è semantica. È terapeutica.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "I pattern sono dinamici, contestuali, modificabili. Le etichette sembrano fisse. La differenza non è semantica. È terapeutica. Patterns are dynamic, contextual, changeable. Labels seem fixed. The difference is not semantic. It is therapeutic. Info & reflection: It reframes personality tests (Myers-Briggs, Enneagram): useful as a starting point, dangerous as an end point. Patterns can be changed; labels you end up putting up with."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-sovrastruttura-fondamenta.html", "t": "Qualsiasi cambiamento rischia di essere una sovrastruttura costruita su fondamenta che non conosci. Regge finché non arriva la prima tempesta seria.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "Qualsiasi cambiamento rischia di essere una sovrastruttura costruita su fondamenta che non conosci. Regge finché non arriva la prima tempesta seria. Any change risks being a superstructure built on foundations you don't know. It holds until the first serious storm comes. Info & reflection: It explains why the work on identity (knowing where you start from) comes before any change that lasts. Invisible foundations hold until real pressure comes. It's the practical answer to the question \"why don't my changes last?\""}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/celli-trasformazione-non-sostituisce.html", "t": "Il cambiamento ha una logica binaria, un prima e un dopo separati da una discontinuità netta. La trasformazione funziona diversamente. Non sostituisce. Incorpora, espande, complessifica.", "b": "Enzo Celli", "x": "Il cambiamento ha una logica binaria, un prima e un dopo separati da una discontinuità netta. La trasformazione funziona diversamente. Non sostituisce. Incorpora, espande, complessifica. Change has a binary logic, a before and an after separated by a clean break. Transformation works differently. It doesn't replace. It takes in, expands, makes more complex. Info & reflection: It changes the point from which I look at every change: I'm not losing who I was, I'm including who I was in something wider. The caterpillar doesn't disappear into the butterfly; it is brought to completion."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/charms-versi-vetro.html", "t": "Poems should be written in such a way that if you throw a poem at a window, the glass will break.", "b": "Daniil Charms", "x": "Poems should be written in such a way that if you throw a poem at a window, the glass will break. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Visceri sul tavolo Info & reflection: poetry as a physical, material object, with weight and force of impact. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Kharms in the chapter Visceri sul tavolo: verses that can break a window, the life-or-death urgency Brondi claims for himself"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/ciseri-ecce-homo.html", "t": "Ecce Homo", "b": "Antonio Ciseri", "x": "Commissioned in 1871 by the Italian government and finished in 1891, the year Ciseri died. Oil on canvas, 298 x 396 cm. Source: Web Gallery of Art , Wikipedia ."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/climaco-freccia-bersaglio.html", "t": "It is that same arrow, first aimed at the wrong target, that once the right aim is understood drives us to the goal.", "b": "John Climacus", "x": "It is that same arrow, first aimed at the wrong target, that once the right aim is understood drives us to the goal. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — L'animale che mi porto dentro Info & reflection: the same force that destroys can save: it's not about changing it but about redirecting it. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — John Climacus in the chapter L'animale che mi porto dentro: the passions have to be redirected and sublimated so they give light instead of burning Addiction is proof that you're capable of intense devotion. You are just serving the wrong god. — \"serving the wrong god\" is Climacus's misdirected arrow: same energy, different target"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/confucio-tre-modi-saggezza.html", "t": "By three methods we may learn wisdom: First, by reflection, which is noblest; Second, by imitation, which is easiest; and Third by experience, which is the bitterest.", "b": "Confucius (attr.)", "x": "\"By three methods we may learn wisdom: First, by reflection, which is noblest; Second, by imitation, which is easiest; and Third by experience, which is the bitterest.\" Info & reflection: The structure is interesting whatever the attribution: three paths ranked by nobility, not by ease. Reflection is the noblest because it needs no outside push: you create the movement yourself. Imitation is the easiest because you follow. Experience is the bitterest because you have to go through it to learn. ⚠️ Apocryphal attribution: this quote goes around everywhere as Confucius, Analects , but it doesn't match any passage of the Analects. The oldest documented source is James Wood's Dictionary of Quotations (1893), a Western work. The Analects passage that actually matches is 16:9 : 生而知之者，上也；學而知之者，次也；困而學之，又其次也 (\"Those born with knowledge are the highest; those who acquire it by study come next; those who acquire it through hardship come after them.\") The Western paraphrase turns \"innate knowledge\" into \"reflection\", which is a big liberty. Sinologists class it as a free paraphrase that \"speaks through Confucius's mouth\". Verdict: apocryphal."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/cronenberg-videodrome-media-identita.html", "t": "Una riflessione visionaria su come i mezzi di comunicazione possano influenzare la nostra identità, i nostri desideri e perfino la nostra percezione della realtà.", "b": "David Cronenberg", "x": "Una riflessione visionaria su come i mezzi di comunicazione possano influenzare la nostra identità, i nostri desideri e perfino la nostra percezione della realtà. A visionary reflection on how the media can shape our identity, our desires and even our perception of reality. Info & reflection: Videodrome (1983) is about manipulation, addiction to images, reality falling apart, communication turned into a tool of control. More than forty years later it still looks less like a fantasy and more like a forecast."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/dali-prospettiva.html", "t": "Cristo di San Giovanni della Croce", "b": "Salvador Dalí", "x": "It's wonderful, very moving, for the new perspective it offers. Related in anthology The Calling of Saint Matthew — Same kind of sacred art that reinterprets a moment in Christ's life visually from an unusual angle."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/dalla-piacere-agli-altri.html", "t": "Dalla si sedeva sempre al tavolino piú esterno, esposto al passaggio. In realtà non vedeva l'ora che qualcuno si avvicinasse a salutarlo. Diceva, apparentemente scherzando, che non puoi piacere agli altri se gli altri non piacciono a te.", "b": "Lucio Dalla", "x": "Dalla si sedeva sempre al tavolino piú esterno, esposto al passaggio. In realtà non vedeva l'ora che qualcuno si avvicinasse a salutarlo. Diceva, apparentemente scherzando, che non puoi piacere agli altri se gli altri non piacciono a te. Dalla always sat at the outermost table, in full view of passers-by. In truth he couldn't wait for someone to come over and say hello. He used to say, seemingly as a joke, that you can't be liked by others if you don't like others. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Tutti assieme Info & reflection: the last line turns everything around: it's not a technique for being likeable, it's about being genuinely turned toward other people. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Dalla's anecdote is in the chapter Tutti assieme, next to Jung's idea of art as an antidote to the illusion of being separate"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/deandre-dita-chitarra.html", "t": "Pensavo è bello che dove finiscono le mie dita / debba in qualche modo incominciare una chitarra.", "b": "Fabrizio De André", "x": "Pensavo è bello che dove finiscono le mie dita debba in qualche modo incominciare una chitarra. I was thinking how beautiful it is that where my fingers end, a guitar should somehow begin. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Insistere Info & reflection: the instrument as an extension of the body. The border between self and art as a zone of continuity. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes it in the Insistere chapter: the instrument as an extension of the body, the physical practice from which, for him, the unconscious comes out"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/deandre-spazio-tempo.html", "t": "In una società ossessionata dal concetto di tempo non si tiene conto dello spazio.", "b": "Fabrizio De André", "x": "In una società ossessionata dal concetto di tempo non si tiene conto dello spazio. In a society obsessed with the concept of time, no one takes space into account. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Una stanza tutta per sé Info & reflection: the obsession with time as blindness to space, and creative practice needs both. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes De André in the chapter Una stanza tutta per sé: creative practice needs a dedicated physical space, as well as time"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/deleuze-nulla-da-dire.html", "t": "So it's not a problem of getting people to express themselves but of providing little gaps of solitude and silence in which they might eventually find something to say. Repressive forces don't stop people expressing themselves but rather force them to express themselves; what a relief to have nothing to say, the right to say nothing, because only then is there a chance of framing the rare, and ever rarer, thing that might be worth saying.", "b": "Gilles Deleuze", "x": "So it's not a problem of getting people to express themselves but of providing little gaps of solitude and silence in which they might eventually find something to say. Repressive forces don't stop people expressing themselves but rather force them to express themselves; what a relief to have nothing to say, the right to say nothing, because only then is there a chance of framing the rare, and ever rarer, thing that might be worth saying. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Urlare senza suono Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes Deleuze in Urlare senza suono to defend his own long silences: the right to have nothing to say"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/don-miguel-ruiz-non-prenderla-sul-personale.html", "t": "Whatever happens around you, don't take it personally... Nothing other people do is because of you. It is because of themselves.", "b": "Don Miguel Ruiz", "x": "\"Whatever happens around you, don't take it personally... Nothing other people do is because of you. It is because of themselves.\" Related in anthology People who can't Communicate think everything is an Argument , and people who lack Accountability think everything is an Attack . — Practical use in communication: not taking every piece of feedback as an attack. Be impeccable with your word. Don't take anything personally. Don't make assumptions. Always do your best. — The same Toltec agreement, in the wider summary of Ruiz's four principles."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/don-miguel-ruiz-parola-impeccabile.html", "t": "Be impeccable with your word. Don't take anything personally. Don't make assumptions. Always do your best.", "b": "Don Miguel Ruiz", "x": "\"Be impeccable with your word. Don't take anything personally. Don't make assumptions. Always do your best.\" Info & reflection: the four Toltec agreements in one line, a practical rule for everyday conduct. Related in anthology Whatever happens around you, don't take it personally... Nothing other people do is because of you. It is because of themselves. — One of the four agreements summed up here, explored in a quote of its own."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/dont-be-the-person-who-never-tried.html", "t": "Being a failed version of who you want to be is still infinitely cooler than being a person who never tried because they were afraid of looking stupid.", "b": "", "x": "Being a failed version of who you want to be is still infinitely cooler than being a person who never tried because they were afraid of looking stupid. The anyway mindset means separating your identity from your results. If the work is bad today, it doesn't mean you are bad. It just means the work is bad. So, you do it again. And again. Breakthroughs are just the logical conclusion of a thousand anyway moments piled on top of each other until the dam finally breaks. — the \"thousand anyway moments\" before the breakthrough are the middle phase: progress you can't see, and process metrics instead of result metrics"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/dostoevskij-tolleranza-imbecilli.html", "t": "Tolerance will reach such a level that intelligent people will be banned for thinking so as not to offend the imbeciles.", "b": "Fyodor Dostoevsky (attr., apocryphal)", "x": "\"Tolerance will reach such a level that intelligent people will be banned for thinking so as not to offend the imbeciles.\" Info & reflection: It catches a real tension: the moment when sensitivity to offence becomes so widespread that it silences serious thinking. Tolerance as a tool of social conformity, used not to protect the vulnerable but to make disagreement intolerable. The idea is worth keeping whoever said it. ⚠️ Apocryphal attribution: this quote does not appear in any work by Dostoevsky. Shown to be false by Snopes, Lead Stories and Misbar. Memes with this sentence appeared in July 2021 on social media (at first in anti-vax contexts); in Russian they circulated paired with the TV film Heart of a Dog (1988, from Bulgakov), but the sentence is in neither the original text nor the screenplay. Origin: anonymous, modern. Classification: apocryphal."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/dostoevsky-peccato-maschera-liberta.html", "t": "«Sin wears the mask of freedom until the soul asks to be free. Only then does the prison reveal itself.»", "b": "Fyodor Dostoevsky", "x": "«Sin wears the mask of freedom until the soul asks to be free. Only then does the prison reveal itself.» Note: quote attributed to The Brothers Karamazov; it may be a modern paraphrase of the theme, not a verbatim passage."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/dsouza-castita-consacrazione-potere.html", "t": "For the early Church, chastity wasn't simply about \"saving yourself for marriage.\" It was about consecrating yourself — body and soul — to something higher than the earthly authority of powerful people and powerful systems.", "b": "Tanika D'Souza", "x": "For the early Church, chastity wasn't simply about \"saving yourself for marriage.\" It was about consecrating yourself — body and soul — to something higher than the earthly authority of powerful people and powerful systems. Info & reflection: It flips the purity culture frame: chastity as an act of power and consecration, not of shame or conformity. The authority you step away from isn't abstract: it's \"powerful people and powerful systems.\""}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/duras-scrittura-selvatica.html", "t": "Writing makes us wild. It takes us back to a wildness from before life. And we always recognize it: it's the wildness of the forests, as old as the world. The wildness of the fear of everything, distinct from life itself and inseparable from it. [...] It isn't only writing, the written word, it's the cries of the night animals, everyone's cries, yours and mine, the dogs' cries.", "b": "Marguerite Duras", "x": "Writing makes us wild. It takes us back to a wildness from before life. And we always recognize it: it's the wildness of the forests, as old as the world. The wildness of the fear of everything, distinct from life itself and inseparable from it. [...] It isn't only writing, the written word, it's the cries of the night animals, everyone's cries, yours and mine, the dogs' cries. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Città contemporanee e foreste primitive Info & reflection: writing as a return to something from before civilization. Not expression but emission. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes Duras in Città contemporanee e foreste primitive: wild writing like the primeval forest Thoreau sees under every city"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/eckhart-vuoto-sole.html", "t": "God does not tolerate emptiness, neither in nature nor in the soul. He cannot help shining out everywhere, without distinction. \"Where and when he finds you ready [that is, empty],\" writes Meister Eckhart, \"he must act and pour himself into you, just as the sun cannot help pouring itself out, and nothing can hold it back, when the air is clear and pure.", "b": "Meister Eckhart", "x": "God does not tolerate emptiness, neither in nature nor in the soul. He cannot help shining out everywhere, without distinction. \"Where and when he finds you ready [that is, empty],\" writes Meister Eckhart, \"he must act and pour himself into you, just as the sun cannot help pouring itself out, and nothing can hold it back, when the air is clear and pure.\" via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Urlare senza suono Info & reflection: emptiness as an active condition, not a passive one: getting ready to receive is already an act. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi takes up Eckhart in Urlare senza suono: making yourself empty is already an act, the condition for God to pour himself out like the sun"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/epicoco-beni-spirituali.html", "t": "Seppelliti dal consumismo, abbiamo fatto incetta di tanti beni materiali, ma non sappiamo piú dove procurarci beni spirituali. E quando usiamo la parola «spirituale» non ci riferiamo a qualche sottoprodotto utile come antidolorifico per le nostre ferite psicologiche, ma a qualcosa che faccia da olio alla fiamma della passione per la vita, che dovrebbe essere il vero motore del mondo.", "b": "Luigi Maria Epicoco", "x": "Seppelliti dal consumismo, abbiamo fatto incetta di tanti beni materiali, ma non sappiamo piú dove procurarci beni spirituali. E quando usiamo la parola «spirituale» non ci riferiamo a qualche sottoprodotto utile come antidolorifico per le nostre ferite psicologiche, ma a qualcosa che faccia da olio alla fiamma della passione per la vita, che dovrebbe essere il vero motore del mondo. Buried by consumerism, we have stocked up on so many material goods, but we no longer know where to get spiritual goods. And when we use the word \"spiritual\" we don't mean some by-product that is useful as a painkiller for our psychological wounds, but something that acts as oil for the flame of the passion for life, which should be the real engine of the world. Il materialismo è un modo di «addomesticare la disperazione», ma non ci riesce mai. Materialism is a way of \"taming despair\", but it never succeeds. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Introduzione Info & reflection: the difference between the spiritual as a painkiller and the spiritual as fuel for the passion for life. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — from the introduction: the spiritual as oil for the passion for living, against materialism, the same thesis Brondi starts from"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/eraclito-fiume-divenire.html", "t": "No man ever steps in the same river twice, for it is not the same river and he is not the same man.", "b": "Heraclitus", "x": "\"No man ever steps in the same river twice, for it is not the same river and he is not the same man.\" Info & reflection: The metaphor rests on two movements at once, not one: the river changes (the object), but whoever steps in is already someone else too (the subject). It isn't just \"everything flows\": the observer is not a fixed point from which to measure change. Every encounter, every rereading, every return to a thought is already a meeting between two things that are different from before. Attribution: the idea is Heraclitus's (fragments B12 and B91), but \"and he is not the same man\" is a later paraphrase, not his words."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/fellini-arte-miracolo.html", "t": "Io credo che l'arte sia questo: la possibilità di trasformare il fallimento in vittoria, la tristezza in felicità: l'arte è il miracolo.", "b": "Federico Fellini", "x": "Io credo che l'arte sia questo: la possibilità di trasformare il fallimento in vittoria, la tristezza in felicità: l'arte è il miracolo. I believe this is what art is: the chance to turn failure into victory, sadness into happiness: art is the miracle. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — L'arte di risorgere Info & reflection: art doesn't document failure, it transforms it. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the chapter L'arte di risorgere trauma is pushed out through creativity and given back to the world as something beautiful, Fellini's miracle"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/fellini-sassolino.html", "t": "Io sono ignorante, ma ho letto qualche libro. Tu non ci crederai, ma tutto quello che c'è a questo mondo serve a qualcosa. Ecco, prendi quel sasso lì, per esempio. Quale? Questo, uno qualunque. Beh, anche questo serve a qualcosa, anche questo sassetto. E a cosa serve? Serve... Ma che ne so io? Se lo sapessi sai chi sarei? Chi? Il padre eterno che sa tutto. Quando nasci, quando mori. E chi può saperlo? No. Non lo so a cosa serve questo sasso lì. Ma a qualcosa deve servire. Perché se questo è inutile, allora è inutile tutto. Anche le stelle.", "b": "Federico Fellini", "x": "Io sono ignorante, ma ho letto qualche libro. Tu non ci crederai, ma tutto quello che c'è a questo mondo serve a qualcosa. Ecco, prendi quel sasso lì, per esempio. Quale? Questo, uno qualunque. Beh, anche questo serve a qualcosa, anche questo sassetto. E a cosa serve? Serve... Ma che ne so io? Se lo sapessi sai chi sarei? Chi? Il padre eterno che sa tutto. Quando nasci, quando mori. E chi può saperlo? No. Non lo so a cosa serve questo sasso lì. Ma a qualcosa deve servire. Perché se questo è inutile, allora è inutile tutto. Anche le stelle. I'm ignorant, but I've read a few books. You won't believe it, but everything in this world is good for something. Here, take that stone there, for example. Which one? This one, any one. Well, even this one is good for something, even this little pebble. And what is it good for? It's good for... How should I know? If I knew, you know who I'd be? Who? The Almighty, who knows everything. When you're born, when you die. And who can know that? No. I don't know what this stone is good for. But it must be good for something. Because if this is useless, then everything is useless. Even the stars. Il Matto (Richard Basehart) to Gelsomina (Giulietta Masina), the pebble scene, La strada (1954) Info & reflection: Gelsomina has just said \"I'm no use to anyone\". The pebble is his reply, but it isn't the answer. Il Matto doesn't say \"this is what you're for\": he refuses to answer that question and puts another in its place: if you can prove this stone is useless, then you admit everything is useless. The reductio ad absurdum climbs up to the stars. The genius of the argument is that it shifts the burden of proof: you don't have to prove you're good for something; it's the claim of uselessness that has to hold up, and it doesn't. The purpose exists even when it's invisible, even when not even God knows it. Related in anthology La Strada — film note with the full plot and critical context"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/ferrante-vero-e-veramente.html", "t": "«Ti posso far notare una cosa? Usi sempre vero e veramente, sia quando parli che quando scrivi. Oppure dici: all'improvviso. Ma quando mai la gente parla veramente e quando mai le cose succedono all'improvviso? Lo sai meglio di me che è tutto un imbroglio e che a una cosa ne segue un'altra e un'altra ancora. Io non faccio più niente veramente, Lenù. E alle cose ho imparato a starci attenta, solo i cretini credono che succedono all'improvviso.»", "b": "Elena Ferrante", "x": "«Ti posso far notare una cosa? Usi sempre vero e veramente, sia quando parli che quando scrivi. Oppure dici: all'improvviso. Ma quando mai la gente parla veramente e quando mai le cose succedono all'improvviso? Lo sai meglio di me che è tutto un imbroglio e che a una cosa ne segue un'altra e un'altra ancora. Io non faccio più niente veramente, Lenù. E alle cose ho imparato a starci attenta, solo i cretini credono che succedono all'improvviso.» \"Can I point something out to you? You always use true and truly, both when you talk and when you write. Or you say: suddenly. But when do people ever talk truly, and when do things ever happen suddenly? You know better than I do that it's all a con and that one thing follows another and then another. I don't do anything truly anymore, Lenù. And I've learned to keep an eye on things; only idiots believe they happen suddenly.\" (myriibooks, caption): \"Lila Cerullo and her ruthless truths\". Lila takes apart Lenù's language (\"true and truly\", \"suddenly\") as the symptom of a way of telling yourself the world that hides the effort of control: nothing really happens suddenly, everything is a chain of causes, and only someone who fools themselves otherwise can afford the innocence of chance."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/ferrante-versi-tranquillanti.html", "t": "«Evocai versi e romanzi come tranquillanti. Forse, pensai, aver studiato mi serve solo a questo: a calmarmi.»", "b": "Elena Ferrante", "x": "«Evocai versi e romanzi come tranquillanti. Forse, pensai, aver studiato mi serve solo a questo: a calmarmi.» \"I called up poems and novels like tranquillizers. Maybe, I thought, this is all my studying is good for: calming me down.\" (Margherita Bienne, caption): In Ferrante, studying is one of the few ways to build some distance from where you come from. But that distance doesn't automatically mean freedom: often it serves to keep a balance, to stop your surroundings from overwhelming you completely. A use of literature that has little of the abstract and a lot of the concrete, almost physiological."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/frankl-spazio-stimolo-risposta.html", "t": "Between stimulus and response there is a space. In that space is our power to choose our response. In our response lies our growth and our freedom.", "b": "Viktor Frankl", "x": "Between stimulus and response there is a space. In that space is our power to choose our response. In our response lies our growth and our freedom. Info & reflection: the pause as an act of freedom: not passivity, but the moment when the automatic reaction becomes a conscious choice. It applies to the nervous system, to emotions, to any impulse. Attribution: uncertain. The line is not in Man's Search for Meaning ; Stephen Covey made it famous and said he didn't remember where he had read it. Related in anthology The simple act of paying attention can take you a long way. — Another quote on the same idea: attention as a conscious pause before reacting."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/galsworthy-idealismo-distanza.html", "t": "Idealism increases in direct proportion to one's distance from the problem.", "b": "John Galsworthy", "x": "Idealism increases in direct proportion to one's distance from the problem. Attribution: uncertain. It only turns up in quote collections, with no reference to a work by Galsworthy."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/ghirri-noia-necessaria.html", "t": "Dove il tempo è segnato da una noia necessaria.", "b": "Luigi Ghirri", "x": "Dove il tempo è segnato da una noia necessaria. Where time is marked by a necessary boredom. Boredom is the threshold to great deeds. — Walter Benjamin, quoted together with Ghirri via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Una noia necessaria Info & reflection: boredom as a threshold, not an absence: something to protect and claim. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the chapter Una noia necessaria Brondi puts Ghirri and Benjamin side by side: boredom as a threshold, inside his praise of doing nothing"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/ghirri-rispetto-irrilevante.html", "t": "Il mio rispetto per le cose irrilevanti sta assumendo proporzioni gigantesche.", "b": "Luigi Ghirri", "x": "Il mio rispetto per le cose irrilevanti sta assumendo proporzioni gigantesche. My respect for irrelevant things is taking on gigantic proportions. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Viaggi di tre chilometri Info & reflection: attention to irrelevant things as an artistic and spiritual practice. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Ghirri in the chapter Viaggi di tre chilometri: respect for irrelevant things as an artistic and a spiritual practice at once The act of seeing is no small thing. To see something is to be possessed by it. Sometimes it carries off a part of you, sometimes it's your whole soul. — Kajii: the act of seeing possesses the one who looks, the root of a gaze that respects the irrelevant."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/gilbert-intervalli.html", "t": "Our lives happen between / the memorable.", "b": "Jack Gilbert", "x": "Our lives happen between the memorable. But the best is often when nothing is happening. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Quando non succede niente Info & reflection: real life is in the gaps, not in the events. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes the poem in the chapter Quando non succede niente, where art and meditation are celebrations of the moments we overlook"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/giossi-dondero-avanti-e-di-lato.html", "t": "Dondero camminava in avanti e di lato, in avanti per muoversi e di lato per incontrare le persone.", "b": "Giacomo Giossi", "x": "Dondero camminava in avanti e di lato, in avanti per muoversi e di lato per incontrare le persone. Dondero walked forward and sideways: forward to move, and sideways to meet people. Info & reflection: the geometry of walking as a metaphor for an attitude toward the world: movement always has two directions, your own path and openness to the other."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/giossi-felicita-nel-bailamme.html", "t": "È lì in quel preciso momento in cui gli studenti ti corrono a fianco per evitare l'ennesimo ritardo, in cui l'avvocato strilla al cellulare alle tue spalle e l'anziana rallenta nel punto in cui sei costretto a fermarti e aspettare che passi, è proprio lì che bisognerebbe provare a essere felici.", "b": "Giacomo Giossi", "x": "È lì in quel preciso momento in cui gli studenti ti corrono a fianco per evitare l'ennesimo ritardo, in cui l'avvocato strilla al cellulare alle tue spalle e l'anziana rallenta nel punto in cui sei costretto a fermarti e aspettare che passi, è proprio lì che bisognerebbe provare a essere felici. It's right there, at that precise moment when students run past you to avoid being late yet again, when the lawyer shouts into his phone behind you and the old woman slows down just where you're forced to stop and wait for her to pass, it's right there that we should try to be happy. Info & reflection: happiness is not in a quiet carved out of the world, but exactly in the moment of greatest intrusion: the chaos of the city as a condition, not an obstacle."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/giossi-gambe-in-un-posto-mente-altrove.html", "t": "È il modo migliore che io conosca per stare con le gambe in un posto e con la mente da tutt'altra parte ed è anche il modo meno maleducato di distrarmi.", "b": "Giacomo Giossi", "x": "È il modo migliore che io conosca per stare con le gambe in un posto e con la mente da tutt'altra parte ed è anche il modo meno maleducato di distrarmi. It is the best way I know to have my legs in one place and my mind somewhere else entirely, and it is also the least rude way to let my mind wander. Info & reflection: the most precise definition of what walking does to the mind. A physical presence and a mental absence that, oddly, is not rudeness but freedom."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/goldratt-esperto-domanda.html", "t": "An expert is not someone who gives you the answer, it is someone who asks you the right question.", "b": "Eliyahu M. Goldratt", "x": "An expert is not someone who gives you the answer, it is someone who asks you the right question. Related in anthology My favorite form of intimacy has always been asking questions, not to pry, but to understand the architecture of someone's inner world. The way they think. The way they feel. The way their past still echoes in their present. There's a depth in people that never shows unless someone asks the kind of questions that require honesty instead of performance. What shaped you. What steadied you. What you learned the hard way. What you still carry even when you pretend you don't. — The value of the right question, here in a relational key."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/han-noia-profonda.html", "t": "If sleep represents the high point of bodily relaxation, deep boredom is the peak of mental relaxation. A purely hectic rush produces nothing new. It reproduces and accelerates what is already available.", "b": "Byung-Chul Han", "x": "If sleep represents the high point of bodily relaxation, deep boredom is the peak of mental relaxation. A purely hectic rush produces nothing new. It reproduces and accelerates what is already available. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Una noia necessaria Info & reflection: deep boredom as the condition for creating, not its absence. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Han in the chapter Una noia necessaria: deep boredom as spiritual rest, the doing nothing that the book's title claims"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/han-prodotto-di-un-luogo.html", "t": "We are all the product of a place, that is, of a set of conditionings: our social and family background, language, history, culture, genes... And these conditionings shape our individual consciousness, its thoughts, words, actions, secrets, contradictions. A philosopher is all the more a philosopher the more he resists the coercive force the place exerts. He doesn't drink from the spirit of the age. [...] He goes and gets his inspiration where it is: far from the crowd.", "b": "Byung-Chul Han", "x": "We are all the product of a place, that is, of a set of conditionings: our social and family background, language, history, culture, genes... And these conditionings shape our individual consciousness, its thoughts, words, actions, secrets, contradictions. A philosopher is all the more a philosopher the more he resists the coercive force the place exerts. He doesn't drink from the spirit of the age. [...] He goes and gets his inspiration where it is: far from the crowd. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Fuori luogo Info & reflection: the force of place as coercion, and resisting it as the condition for thinking. Attribution: uncertain. Brondi gives it as Byung-Chul Han's, but the passage can't be traced to any of his books. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Han in the chapter Fuori luogo: the thinker resists the coercion of place, the way Brondi's artist resists the logic of the market"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/han-silenzio-sacro.html", "t": "The sacred is a *phenomenon of silence*. It makes us prick up our ears. […] Hypercommunication, the noise of communication, desecrates the world, profanes it. No one is *listening*. […] Silence sharpens our attention to the *higher order*.", "b": "Byung-Chul Han", "x": "The sacred is a phenomenon of silence . It makes us prick up our ears. […] Hypercommunication, the noise of communication, desecrates the world, profanes it. No one is listening . […] Silence sharpens our attention to the higher order . via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Urlare senza suono Info & reflection: the sacred isn't content but a condition: silence as the precondition for listening. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes Han in Urlare senza suono, where he defends his long silences between one record and the next against the noise of communication Nothing, however, will help you so much as keeping still—talking very little with others, and as much as may be with yourself. — parallel structure: silence as the condition for listening to the higher order (Han) and for talking with yourself (Seneca); from different traditions, the same diagnosis against noise"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/harrison-coscienza.html", "t": "I just changed my consciousness, that's all.", "b": "George Harrison", "x": "I just changed my consciousness, that's all. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — epigraph Info & reflection: inner change as a more radical act than any outward change. Attribution: uncertain. Brondi uses it as an epigraph; it can't be traced to an interview or a song by Harrison. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — the book's epigraph: changing your own consciousness as the gesture that starts all of Brondi's vertical search"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/hillesum-aiutare-dio.html", "t": "And with almost every heartbeat my certainty grows: You cannot help us, but we must help You and defend Your dwelling place inside us to the last. There are, it is true, some who, even at this last stage, are putting their vacuum cleaners and silver forks and spoons in safekeeping instead of guarding You, dear God.", "b": "Etty Hillesum", "x": "And with almost every heartbeat my certainty grows: You cannot help us, but we must help You and defend Your dwelling place inside us to the last. There are, it is true, some who, even at this last stage, are putting their vacuum cleaners and silver forks and spoons in safekeeping instead of guarding You, dear God. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Proteggere Dio Info & reflection: the radical reversal. Not God protecting us, but us protecting God in us. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Etty Hillesum in the Proteggere Dio chapter: it is up to us to keep God safe in us, while others save the silverware"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/hunt-light-world.html", "t": "The Light of the World", "b": "William Holman Hunt", "x": "This is the first version, at Keble College since 1873. Hunt painted two more: a smaller one (1851–1856) at Manchester Art Gallery and a life-size one (1900–1904) at St Paul's Cathedral, London. Source: Wikipedia ."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/idc-vulnerabilità.html", "t": "Mi ricordo il momento esatto in cui ho deciso che sì, potevi farmi male", "b": "anonymous", "x": "Mi ricordo il momento esatto in cui ho deciso che sì, potevi farmi male I remember the exact moment I decided that yes, you could hurt me"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/jouvenel-pecore-lupi.html", "t": "Sooner or later, a society of sheep must create a government of wolves.", "b": "Bertrand de Jouvenel", "x": "Sooner or later, a society of sheep must create a government of wolves. Note on attribution: Common attribution, but not traced to a specific work by de Jouvenel."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/jung-pensiero-difficile.html", "t": "Thinking is difficult, therefore let the herd pronounce judgment!", "b": "Carl Gustav Jung", "x": "Thinking is difficult, therefore let the herd pronounce judgment! Simplified version going around online (not verified): \"Thinking is difficult. That's why most people judge.\""}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/jung-sogni-natura.html", "t": "We are so captivated by and entangled in our subjective consciousness that we have forgotten the age-old fact that God speaks chiefly through dreams and visions.", "b": "Carl Gustav Jung", "x": "We are so captivated by and entangled in our subjective consciousness that we have forgotten the age-old fact that God speaks chiefly through dreams and visions. Man feels himself isolated in the cosmos, because he is no longer involved in nature and has lost his emotional \"unconscious identity\" with natural phenomena. These have slowly lost their symbolic implications. Thunder is no longer the voice of an angry god, nor is lightning his avenging missile. […] No voices now speak to man from stones, plants, and animals, nor does he speak to them believing they can hear. […] This enormous loss is compensated by the symbols in our dreams. They bring up our original nature: its instincts and peculiar thinking. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — La vita onirica Info & reflection: dreams as compensation for cosmic isolation, and art as the only possible translator. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Jung in the chapter on dream life: dreams make up for man's isolation from nature, and Brondi gives art the job of translating them"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/kafka-ascia-ghiaccio.html", "t": "But we need the books that affect us like a disaster, that grieve us deeply, like the death of someone we loved more than ourselves, like being banished into forests far from everyone, like a suicide. A book must be the axe for the frozen sea inside us.", "b": "Franz Kafka", "x": "But we need the books that affect us like a disaster, that grieve us deeply, like the death of someone we loved more than ourselves, like being banished into forests far from everyone, like a suicide. A book must be the axe for the frozen sea inside us. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Visceri sul tavolo Info & reflection: the book as a tool for breaking, not for comfort. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes Kafka in Visceri sul tavolo: the book as an axe that breaks the ice, close to his own experiment with truth that unsettles"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/kafka-primo-segno-comprensione.html", "t": "A first sign of the beginning of understanding is the wish to die.", "b": "Franz Kafka", "x": "A first sign of the beginning of understanding is the wish to die. Info & reflection: real understanding doesn't bring relief, it brings the weight of seeing. The wish to die as the first symptom of clarity: Kafka turns around the idea that understanding means feeling better."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/kajii-atto-di-vedere.html", "t": "The act of seeing is no small thing. To see something is to be possessed by it. Sometimes it carries off a part of you, sometimes it's your whole soul.", "b": "Motojirō Kajii", "x": "The act of seeing is no small thing. To see something is to be possessed by it. Sometimes it carries off a part of you, sometimes it's your whole soul. Related in anthology Il mio rispetto per le cose irrilevanti sta assumendo proporzioni gigantesche. — Seeing possesses the one who looks: Ghirri brings the same attention to the detail thought irrelevant. Abstract artwork (untitled) — Being possessed by what you see: Lysak's light is a concrete case."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/kerouac-profondita.html", "t": "Blow as deep as you want—write as deeply, fish as far down as you want, satisfy yourself first, then reader cannot fail to receive telepathic shock.", "b": "Jack Kerouac", "x": "Blow as deep as you want—write as deeply, fish as far down as you want, satisfy yourself first, then reader cannot fail to receive telepathic shock. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Shock telepatico Info & reflection: real depth gets across directly. It isn't technique, it's transmission. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — It's the source of Brondi's telepathic shock: whoever goes deep and tells the truth passes it on to people with the same sensitivity"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/lacecla-insistenza.html", "t": "Se vuoi «cambiare la tua vita», devi passare attraverso l'insistenza di una pratica, attraverso l'esercizio spirituale o fisico che essa comporta.", "b": "Franco La Cecla", "x": "Se vuoi «cambiare la tua vita», devi passare attraverso l'insistenza di una pratica, attraverso l'esercizio spirituale o fisico che essa comporta. If you want to \"change your life\", you have to go through the insistence of a practice, through the spiritual or physical exercise it involves. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Insistere Info & reflection: change isn't a decision but the result of insistence: you change through the body, not through the mind. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — La Cecla in the chapter Insistere: you only change your life by repeating a practice, like Brondi's yoga or Murakami's running"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/lessing-tempo-giusto.html", "t": "There is only one way to read, which is to browse in libraries and bookshops, picking up books that attract you, reading only those, dropping them when they bore you, skipping the parts that drag – and never, never reading anything because you feel you ought, or because it is part of a trend or a movement. Remember that the book which bores you when you are twenty or thirty will open doors for you when you are forty or fifty – and vice versa. Don't read a book out of its right time for you.", "b": "Doris Lessing", "x": "\"There is only one way to read, which is to browse in libraries and bookshops, picking up books that attract you, reading only those, dropping them when they bore you, skipping the parts that drag – and never, never reading anything because you feel you ought, or because it is part of a trend or a movement. Remember that the book which bores you when you are twenty or thirty will open doors for you when you are forty or fifty – and vice versa. Don't read a book out of its right time for you.\" Info & reflection: forcing a book at the wrong moment only teaches you to hate it, and you lose it for the moment when it would have mattered. It holds for almost every book, but not for Scripture, which you don't read out of appetite but as a daily commitment: there the right time isn't known in advance, you are present when it comes. Some books wait for you; others you wait for. Related in anthology The man who has not the habit of reading is imprisoned in his immediate world... The moment he takes up a book, he immediately enters a different world. — Reading opens worlds, but only if the book arrives at its time."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/lin-yutang-convinzioni-ristrettezza.html", "t": "The more we justify our beliefs, the more narrow-minded we become.", "b": "Lin Yutang", "x": "\"The more we justify our beliefs, the more narrow-minded we become.\" Info & reflection: It turns around the common idea that arguing for a position makes it more solid and more open to discussion. Instead, every round of justification adds a brick to the defence and narrows the gaps: you end up defending not the idea any more but what you have invested in defending it. Attribution: uncertain. Quote collections credit The Importance of Living (1937); not checked against the book. Related in anthology The need to be right is the sign of a vulgar mind. — Narrow convictions and the need to be right are the same rigidity seen from two angles. No sick man's monstrous dream can be so wild that some philosopher won't say it's true. — Narrow convictions are the ground on which rationalism builds its justifications."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/lin-yutang-lettura-mondi.html", "t": "The man who has not the habit of reading is imprisoned in his immediate world... The moment he takes up a book, he immediately enters a different world.", "b": "Lin Yutang", "x": "\"The man who has not the habit of reading is imprisoned in his immediate world... The moment he takes up a book, he immediately enters a different world.\" Info & reflection: \"Imprisoned\" is the strong word: not reading isn't neutral, it's a kind of confinement, in your own present, your own small neighbourhood of experience, your own repetitions. The book doesn't add information to a world that's already complete, it literally opens a way out of a cell you didn't see as one. Related in anthology We are like books. Most people only see our cover, the minority read only the introduction, many people believe the critics, and only few will know our content. — If reading opens worlds, people are books few read to the end. There is only one way to read, which is to browse in libraries and bookshops, picking up books that attract you, reading only those, dropping them when they bore you, skipping the parts that drag – and never, never reading anything because you feel you ought, or because it is part of a trend or a movement. Remember that the book which bores you when you are twenty or thirty will open doors for you when you are forty or fifty – and vice versa. Don't read a book out of its right time for you. — A book opens worlds only at its right time."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/lorca-duende.html", "t": "A mysterious force that everyone feels and no philosopher has explained\", an unknown force that drives creation. \"It lives in the body, rising from the soles of the feet, because the feet are the roots that connect the individual to the dark forces of nature.\" It expresses itself without going through technique, knowing something more important, and overturning the rules.", "b": "Federico García Lorca", "x": "\"A mysterious force that everyone feels and no philosopher has explained\", an unknown force that drives creation. \"It lives in the body, rising from the soles of the feet, because the feet are the roots that connect the individual to the dark forces of nature.\" It expresses itself without going through technique, knowing something more important, and overturning the rules. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Shock telepatico Info & reflection: the duende rises from the feet: from below, from the earth, not from the mind. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Lorca's duende, quoted in the chapter Shock telepatico: the artistic gesture that starts from below and from the emotional centre, far from technical skill"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/lysak-luce.html", "t": "Abstract artwork (untitled)", "b": "Gregory Lysak", "x": "Beautiful, the light is wonderful, besides of course everything it evokes. Related in anthology The act of seeing is no small thing. To see something is to be possessed by it. Sometimes it carries off a part of you, sometimes it's your whole soul. — Kajii: seeing is being possessed by something, sometimes the whole soul, like this light."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/marie-curie-capire-per-non-temere.html", "t": "Nothing in life is to be feared, it is only to be understood. Now is the time to understand more, so that we may fear less.", "b": "Marie Curie", "x": "\"Nothing in life is to be feared, it is only to be understood. Now is the time to understand more, so that we may fear less.\" Info & reflection: fear as a function of ignorance, not of real danger: understanding is the first form of courage."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/marie-curie-curiosi-delle-idee.html", "t": "Be less curious about people and more curious about ideas.", "b": "Marie Curie", "x": "\"Be less curious about people and more curious about ideas.\" Info & reflection: a reminder against gossip as a lazy form of curiosity: ideas give back more. Related in anthology My favorite form of intimacy has always been asking questions, not to pry, but to understand the architecture of someone's inner world. The way they think. The way they feel. The way their past still echoes in their present. There's a depth in people that never shows unless someone asks the kind of questions that require honesty instead of performance. What shaped you. What steadied you. What you learned the hard way. What you still carry even when you pretend you don't. — Curiosity about other people's ideas is the same intimacy that comes from the right questions."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/markowitz-humans-vs-nature.html", "t": "Over the last few hundred years, we began to see ourselves as separate from the natural world — masters of it, rather than participants in it. / We built systems that prize speed above all else, and in doing so, we lost the most fundamental lesson that nature teaches: speed of growth makes you fragile. The tree that shoots up fastest is the first to fall in a storm. The invasive species that spreads the quickest chokes out everything around it and then collapses when conditions change. / The ecosystems that endure — the ones that survive fire and drought and ice — are the ones that grew slowly, developed deep root systems, and built interdependence with the living things around them.", "b": "Eric Markowitz", "x": "Over the last few hundred years, we began to see ourselves as separate from the natural world — masters of it, rather than participants in it. We built systems that prize speed above all else, and in doing so, we lost the most fundamental lesson that nature teaches: speed of growth makes you fragile. The tree that shoots up fastest is the first to fall in a storm. The invasive species that spreads the quickest chokes out everything around it and then collapses when conditions change. The ecosystems that endure — the ones that survive fire and drought and ice — are the ones that grew slowly, developed deep root systems, and built interdependence with the living things around them. Info & reflection: It matters to me to find a real connection with nature again"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/mattis-prontezza-determinazione.html", "t": "Be polite, be humble and be professional, but always carry a plan to kill everyone in the room.", "b": "James Mattis", "x": "\"Be polite, be humble and be professional, but always carry a plan to kill everyone in the room.\" Info & reflection: The military side isn't mine and I don't share it. What I keep is the mental structure underneath: politeness and humility aren't weakness, they live alongside total readiness, a level of focus and determination that never switches off. Obsession, turned to the positive, isn't aggression: it's never stopping being fully present to what you're doing."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/miller-carattere-sfide.html", "t": "A character is defined by the kinds of challenges he cannot walk away from. And by those he has walked away from that cause him remorse.", "b": "Arthur Miller", "x": "A character is defined by the kinds of challenges he cannot walk away from. And by those he has walked away from that cause him remorse."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/miller-verita-scomoda.html", "t": "No one wants the truth if it is inconvenient.", "b": "Arthur Miller", "x": "No one wants the truth if it is inconvenient. Related in anthology The need to be right is the sign of a vulgar mind. — two ways of betraying the truth: avoiding it when it's inconvenient, or using it to win"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/moon-whispers-communicate-accountability.html", "t": "People who can't *Communicate* think everything is an *Argument*, and people who lack *Accountability* think everything is an *Attack*.", "b": "@themoonwhispers_", "x": "People who can't Communicate think everything is an Argument , and people who lack Accountability think everything is an Attack . Info & reflection: Two mirror-image misreadings. People who can't communicate read every clarification as a fight; people who don't take responsibility read every piece of feedback as an attack. Both come from the same inability: telling the content of the conversation apart from your own personal worth. Related in anthology Whatever happens around you, don't take it personally... Nothing other people do is because of you. It is because of themselves. — Same principle from another tradition: don't take personally what is being communicated."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/morris-concentrarsi-su-meno.html", "t": "It's not always that we need to do more but rather that we need to focus on less.", "b": "Nathan W. Morris", "x": "It's not always that we need to do more but rather that we need to focus on less. Related in anthology Edit your life frequently and ruthlessly. It's your masterpiece after all. — same author: editing your life without mercy"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/morris-modificare-la-vita.html", "t": "Edit your life frequently and ruthlessly. It's your masterpiece after all.", "b": "Nathan W. Morris", "x": "Edit your life frequently and ruthlessly. It's your masterpiece after all. Related in anthology It's not always that we need to do more but rather that we need to focus on less. — same author: cutting what is superfluous as a way of caring for your own life"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/murakami-scrivere-malsano.html", "t": "Basically I agree with the view that writing novels is an unhealthy type of work. […] like it or not, a kind of toxin that lies deep down in all humanity rises to the surface.", "b": "Haruki Murakami", "x": "Basically I agree with the view that writing novels is an unhealthy type of work. […] like it or not, a kind of toxin that lies deep down in all humanity rises to the surface. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Il sistema immunitario dell'anima Info & reflection: writing isn't cathartic in a hygienic sense: it brings to light something poisonous that needs a strong body. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Murakami in the chapter Il sistema immunitario dell'anima: writing pulls out something toxic and you need a strong body to bear it"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/nietzsche-abisso-mostro.html", "t": "He who fights with monsters should be careful lest he thereby become a monster. And if thou gaze long into an abyss, the abyss will also gaze into thee.", "b": "Friedrich Nietzsche", "x": "He who fights with monsters should be careful lest he thereby become a monster. And if thou gaze long into an abyss, the abyss will also gaze into thee. Info & reflection: the abyss isn't outside: it's what wakes up in whoever stares at it too long. The fight against evil uses the same impulses as evil."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/oconnor-fantastico-reale.html", "t": "A thing is fantastic because it is so real, so real that it is fantastic.", "b": "Flannery O'Connor", "x": "A thing is fantastic because it is so real, so real that it is fantastic. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Quando non succede niente Info & reflection: the fantastic isn't the opposite of the real. It's the real at its most intense. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the chapter Quando non succede niente, art and meditation celebrate the moments we overlook: the real so intense it becomes fantastic"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/pann-lim-cio-che-le-macchine-non-possono.html", "t": "The question is not whether machines can do what humans do. The question is whether humans will continue to do what machines cannot.", "b": "Pann Lim", "x": "The question is not whether machines can do what humans do. The question is whether humans will continue to do what machines cannot. Info & reflection: the right question about AI isn't about what machines can do, but about whether humans want to stay human. Related in anthology No.1 Christianity Expert: If You DON'T Believe In a God You NEED to Hear This! — Lennox: machines don't think and don't have qualia, and the real risk is reducing people to machines"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/pavese-amore-davanti-a-se.html", "t": "L'amore ha la virtù di denudare non i due amanti l'uno di fronte all'altro, ma ciascuno dei due davanti a sé.", "b": "Cesare Pavese", "x": "L'amore ha la virtù di denudare non i due amanti l'uno di fronte all'altro, ma ciascuno dei due davanti a sé. Love has the virtue of stripping bare not the two lovers in front of each other, but each of the two in front of themselves. Info & reflection: love is not mutual exposure, it's a mirror of yourself. It puts you face to face with yourself, not with the other."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/pazienza-schiavo-dellarte.html", "t": "Rinascere […] con l'umiltà di chi da sempre è schiavo della propria arte, e non datore di lavoro del proprio talento, è stupendo.", "b": "Andrea Pazienza", "x": "Rinascere […] con l'umiltà di chi da sempre è schiavo della propria arte, e non datore di lavoro del proprio talento, è stupendo. To be reborn […] with the humility of someone who has always been a slave to his art, and not the employer of his talent, is wonderful. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Ho tanta fede in te Info & reflection: slave vs. employer of your talent. The difference between those who serve art and those who use it. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — the line about being a slave to your own art, from the Ho tanta fede in te chapter: serving your talent instead of exploiting it"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/perel-partner-nel-proprio-elemento.html", "t": "I'm most drawn to my partner when I see them in their element — passionate about something, competent. It's when I see my partner as a separate other person: already so familiar, but yet again somewhat mysterious, somewhat unknown, somewhat elusive. In that moment, when they are radiant and in their element, they don't need me. That space between me and the other is where erotic energy lies.", "b": "Esther Perel", "x": "I'm most drawn to my partner when I see them in their element — passionate about something, competent. It's when I see my partner as a separate other person: already so familiar, but yet again somewhat mysterious, somewhat unknown, somewhat elusive. In that moment, when they are radiant and in their element, they don't need me. That space between me and the other is where erotic energy lies. Info & reflection: Perel separates \"attracted to\" from \"drawn to\": the second goes deeper, a force of gravity. The mechanism runs against intuition: attraction doesn't grow with closeness and dependence, it grows with separateness. Seeing your partner in their element, not needing you, brings the mystery back. Eros lives in the gap between yourself and the other, not in union. She names three situations where you're \"drawn to\": (1) the partner in their element, (2) the reunion after an absence, (3) seeing them through other people's eyes. The only gender-specific one: women when he plays with the kids. \"When a woman plays with the kids it's called maternal, not desire.\""}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/ratzinger-odio-occidente.html", "t": "C'è qui un odio di sé dell'Occidente che è strano e che si può considerare solo come qualcosa di patologico; l'Occidente tenta sì in maniera lodevole di aprirsi pieno di comprensione a valori esterni, ma non ama più se stesso; della sua propria storia vede oramai soltanto ciò che è deprecabile e distruttivo, mentre non è più in grado di percepire ciò che è grande e puro. / L'Europa, per sopravvivere, ha bisogno di una nuova — certamente critica e umile — accettazione di se stessa, se essa vuole davvero sopravvivere. / La multiculturalità, che viene continuamente e con passione incoraggiata e favorita, è talvolta soprattutto abbandono e rinnegamento di ciò che è proprio, fuga dalle cose proprie. Ma la multiculturalità non può sussistere senza costanti in comune, senza punti di orientamento a partire dai valori propri.", "b": "Joseph Ratzinger", "x": "C'è qui un odio di sé dell'Occidente che è strano e che si può considerare solo come qualcosa di patologico; l'Occidente tenta sì in maniera lodevole di aprirsi pieno di comprensione a valori esterni, ma non ama più se stesso; della sua propria storia vede oramai soltanto ciò che è deprecabile e distruttivo, mentre non è più in grado di percepire ciò che è grande e puro. L'Europa, per sopravvivere, ha bisogno di una nuova — certamente critica e umile — accettazione di se stessa, se essa vuole davvero sopravvivere. La multiculturalità, che viene continuamente e con passione incoraggiata e favorita, è talvolta soprattutto abbandono e rinnegamento di ciò che è proprio, fuga dalle cose proprie. Ma la multiculturalità non può sussistere senza costanti in comune, senza punti di orientamento a partire dai valori propri. Here we notice a self-hatred in the Western world that is strange and that can be considered pathological; yes, the West is making a praiseworthy attempt to be completely open to understanding foreign values, but it no longer loves itself; from now on it sees in its own history only what is blameworthy and destructive, whereas it is no longer capable of perceiving what is great and pure. In order to survive, Europe needs a new—and certainly a critical and humble—acceptance of itself, that is, if it wants to survive. Multiculturalism, which is continually and passionately encouraged and promoted, is sometimes little more than the abandonment and denial of what is one's own, flight from one's own heritage. But multiculturalism cannot exist without shared constants, without points of reference based on one's own values. (trans. Michael J. Miller)"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/reeves-onde-surf.html", "t": "You can't stop the waves but you can learn to surf.", "b": "Keanu Reeves", "x": "\"You can't stop the waves but you can learn to surf.\" Info & reflection: The metaphor moves control from the context (which stays out of my reach) to skill (which I can build). It isn't a call to passivity but to knowing how to stay inside the movement of things without expecting to stop it."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/reeves-prestare-attenzione.html", "t": "The simple act of paying attention can take you a long way.", "b": "Keanu Reeves", "x": "\"The simple act of paying attention can take you a long way.\" Info & reflection: No philosophical claim, just a practical reminder: attention isn't a rare talent but a simple act, one you can repeat, within anyone's reach. On its own it's already enough to get results, without more elaborate strategies. Related in anthology Between stimulus and response there is a space. In that space is our power to choose our response. In our response lies our growth and our freedom. — The space between stimulus and response is where the attention described here is really practised."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/rumi-dio-preghiera-peccato.html", "t": "The same God that doesn't answer your prayers right away, is the same God that doesn't punish you right after you sin.", "b": "anonymous (attributed to Rumi, unverified)", "x": "The same God that doesn't answer your prayers right away, is the same God that doesn't punish you right after you sin. Info & reflection: The parallel structure says it all: God's slowness is not absence, it's the same patience working in both directions, grace and mercy as one thing. Note on the attribution: The quote circulates on social media attributed to Rumi, but it can't be traced to any Persian text (neither the Masnavi nor the Divan-e Shams). No verse, no page. Probably anonymous: it follows the pattern of false Rumi attributions documented by scholars such as Ibrahim Gamard."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/saintexupery-riti-tempo.html", "t": "And our immemorial rites are in Time what the dwelling is in Space. For it is well that the years should not seem to wear us away and disperse us like a handful of sand; rather they should fulfill us. It is meet that Time should be a building-up.", "b": "Antoine de Saint-Exupéry", "x": "And our immemorial rites are in Time what the dwelling is in Space. For it is well that the years should not seem to wear us away and disperse us like a handful of sand; rather they should fulfill us. It is meet that Time should be a building-up. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Ritualità Info & reflection: ritual as the architecture of time. It turns the flow into a building. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the Ritualità chapter Brondi uses Saint-Exupéry: rites build time the way a house builds space, against the model of the machine that is always on"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/schein-pensiero-linguaggio-percezione.html", "t": "We do not think and talk about what we see; we see what we are able to think and talk about.", "b": "Edgar Schein", "x": "\"We do not think and talk about what we see; we see what we are able to think and talk about.\" Info & reflection: It flips the intuitive order perception → language and replaces it with available language/concepts → perception. We don't see the world and then describe it: the set of concepts we already have decides in advance what we're equipped to notice. It explains why widening the vocabulary of a field (organisational, emotional, cultural) isn't a side exercise but a condition for seeing things that before were simply invisible."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/schopenhauer-intelletto-insocievolezza.html", "t": "The more a man finds within himself, the less he needs from the outside world. A high degree of intellect tends to make a man unsocial.", "b": "Arthur Schopenhauer", "x": "\"The more a man finds within himself, the less he needs from the outside world. A high degree of intellect tends to make a man unsocial.\" Info & reflection: Two ideas in one sentence. The first: inner wealth and outside need are inversely proportional; whoever has a lot inside carries less hunger for what's outside. The second: intellect makes people unsociable not by temperament but by structure; whoever sees more finds it hard to be fully present in conversations that work on a different level. Together they describe a specific kind of solitude that isn't a lack but a surplus."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/schulz-estrema-riluttanza.html", "t": "Estrema Riluttanza", "b": "Charles M. Schulz", "x": "The pun that turns an emotional quality into a proper name. \"When I go away, you know I'm leaving you with Extreme Reluctance\": language folding back on itself and showing something true about how we name things so we can bear them."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/sconosciuto-addiction-devozione.html", "t": "Addiction is proof that you're capable of intense devotion. You are just serving the wrong god.", "b": "@american_psyco", "x": "Addiction is proof that you're capable of intense devotion. You are just serving the wrong god. The same energy you waste on porn, dopamine hits, scrolling, weed, toxic relationships, or overthinking — that fire is your greatest weapon. The difference between a slave and a king is not the capacity for obsession. It's what you choose to obsess over. Master your focus. Channel your addiction into discipline. Serve the right god. Info & reflection: addiction as energy pointed the wrong way, not as a lack of strength: the problem isn't the capacity for obsession, it's the object. It connects straight to the note on doomscrolling: same neurological mechanism, different direction. Related in anthology Il termine greco utilizzato nei Vangeli per designare il peccato deriva dal verbo amartano , che significa letteralmente «fallire il bersaglio» ed è un termine tecnico usato dagli arcieri. […] Gli impulsi passionali, la freccia, sono cosa sacra e buona, il problema è dove miri, quale è il bersaglio a cui tendi, verso che direzione hai scoccato la freccia. — amartano as the wrong target: the same idea in the Gospel tradition — passion is sacred, the problem is the direction It is that same arrow, first aimed at the wrong target, that once the right aim is understood drives us to the goal. — Climacus: the same arrow, once you understand the right aim, drives you to the goal — exactly the logic of the \"right god\""}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/sconosciuto-intimita-domande.html", "t": "My favorite form of intimacy has always been asking questions, not to pry, but to understand the architecture of someone's inner world. The way they think. The way they feel. The way their past still echoes in their present. There's a depth in people that never shows unless someone asks the kind of questions that require honesty instead of performance. What shaped you. What steadied you. What you learned the hard way. What you still carry even when you pretend you don't.", "b": "anonymous", "x": "My favorite form of intimacy has always been asking questions, not to pry, but to understand the architecture of someone's inner world. The way they think. The way they feel. The way their past still echoes in their present. There's a depth in people that never shows unless someone asks the kind of questions that require honesty instead of performance. What shaped you. What steadied you. What you learned the hard way. What you still carry even when you pretend you don't. Related in anthology Be less curious about people and more curious about ideas. — Marie Curie on curiosity about ideas is close to this intimacy made of questions. An expert is not someone who gives you the answer, it is someone who asks you the right question. — the value of the right question, here as a skill"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/seneca-cruelty-weakness.html", "t": "All cruelty springs from weakness.", "b": "Seneca", "x": "All cruelty springs from weakness. Info & reflection: The sentence turns a common assumption around: cruelty is not a sign of strength but of its absence. Whoever harms others acts out of fear, insecurity or lack of self-control, not from a position of real power. In Seneca's Stoic frame, true strength is self-mastery: someone who is really strong has no need to dominate or to cause pain. The tyrant, the bully, the one who humiliates: they act out of unresolved anxiety and a fragility they can't handle any other way. Note: There is also the variant \"all savageness is a sign of weakness\", another translation of the same idea. The exact English wording is a modern rendering of the Latin original. Related in anthology Nothing, however, will help you so much as keeping still—talking very little with others, and as much as may be with yourself. — the inner dialogue Seneca prescribes is the practice that builds the self-mastery whose absence is cruelty"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/seneca-starsene-quieti.html", "t": "Nothing, however, will help you so much as keeping still—talking very little with others, and as much as may be with yourself.", "b": "Seneca", "x": "Nothing, however, will help you so much as keeping still—talking very little with others, and as much as may be with yourself. Info & reflection: The maxim packs everything into a reversal: the most necessary words are the ones you say to yourself, not to others. Keeping still isn't passivity: it's the condition in which inner dialogue becomes possible. Seneca insists on a practice, not an abstraction: less noise outside, more clarity inside. It's the stoic foundation of self-knowledge. Related in anthology All cruelty springs from weakness. — dialogue with yourself builds the self-knowledge whose absence cruelty shows: two complementary maxims in the same Senecan psychology The sacred is a phenomenon of silence . It makes us prick up our ears. […] Hypercommunication, the noise of communication, desecrates the world, profanes it. No one is listening . […] Silence sharpens our attention to the higher order . — parallel structure from different traditions: Han on silence as the condition for listening to a higher order, Seneca on silence as the method of self-knowledge; the same diagnosis against hypercommunication"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/simon-logica-non-cambia-idee.html", "t": "You do not change people's minds by defeating them with logic.", "b": "Herbert A. Simon", "x": "\"You do not change people's minds by defeating them with logic.\" Info & reflection: Consistent with Simon's work on bounded rationality: people don't decide on pure logic, so winning an argument on logic doesn't touch the real mechanism that forms beliefs. A useful reminder against the temptation to \"be right\" in a discussion, thinking the strongest argument is enough. Related in anthology The need to be right is the sign of a vulgar mind. — Camus ties the need to be right to coarseness of mind, not to a search for truth."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/simon-poverta-attenzione.html", "t": "A wealth of information creates a poverty of attention.", "b": "Herbert A. Simon", "x": "\"A wealth of information creates a poverty of attention.\" Info & reflection: A line from 1971 (Simon coined it talking about information overload before the internet) that today sounds almost prophetic. Attention is a scarce resource and doesn't scale with the amount of information available; in fact, the more information there is, the less attention is left for each piece. It's the economic argument behind the whole current talk about the \"attention economy\". Related in anthology Brené Brown: The Algorithms Have Forced Us Into A Hidden Epidemic, This Is The Only Way Out! — Brené Brown on the \"thinking class\" that reads philosophy and history while selling the scroll to everyone else: the capacity for attention becomes power"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/soncini-sinistra-aria-condizionata.html", "t": "«Nello spirito ormai caratteristico della sinistra, quello di negare l'evidenza con l'orwelliana convinzione che se dici con una certa sicumera che le donne possono avere il cazzo poi l'elettorato non crederà ai propri occhi ma alle tue parole, i laburisti hanno ben pensato di dire per anni che l'aria condizionata non serve: basta aprire le finestre e fare un po' di corrente. E bere tanta acqua e non uscire nelle ore più calde.»", "b": "Guia Soncini", "x": "«Nello spirito ormai caratteristico della sinistra, quello di negare l'evidenza con l'orwelliana convinzione che se dici con una certa sicumera che le donne possono avere il cazzo poi l'elettorato non crederà ai propri occhi ma alle tue parole, i laburisti hanno ben pensato di dire per anni che l'aria condizionata non serve: basta aprire le finestre e fare un po' di corrente. E bere tanta acqua e non uscire nelle ore più calde.» \"In what is by now the typical spirit of the left, denying the obvious with the Orwellian belief that if you say with enough swagger that women can have a dick, voters will believe your words rather than their own eyes, Labour thought it wise to say for years that air conditioning isn't needed: just open the windows and get a bit of a draught going. And drink lots of water and don't go out in the hottest hours.\" Related in anthology L'avrete notato anche voi: non c'è una politica di sinistra, sia essa consigliera comunale di Vizzolo Predabissi o capa di partito, senatrice o ex ministra, che quando si parla della Meloni non ritenga urgente rimarcare che quella pericolosa retrograda si fa mettere l'articolo maschile. E, se è il rimprovero che è più sensato ripetere, capite bene che, oltre al mistero del perché alcuni vogliano candidarsi a sindaco, non è chiaro perché chiunque debba un domani votare la sinistra, quando al governo c'è evidentemente una donna perfetta, così perfetta che tutto ciò che hai da rimproverarle è l'articolo determinativo. — Same polemical target for Soncini: the language of the left that denies reality."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/soncini-sinistra-articolo-determinativo.html", "t": "L'avrete notato anche voi: non c'è una politica di sinistra, sia essa consigliera comunale di Vizzolo Predabissi o capa di partito, senatrice o ex ministra, che quando si parla della Meloni non ritenga urgente rimarcare che quella pericolosa retrograda si fa mettere l'articolo maschile. E, se è il rimprovero che è più sensato ripetere, capite bene che, oltre al mistero del perché alcuni vogliano candidarsi a sindaco, non è chiaro perché chiunque debba un domani votare la sinistra, quando al governo c'è evidentemente una donna perfetta, così perfetta che tutto ciò che hai da rimproverarle è l'articolo determinativo.", "b": "Guia Soncini", "x": "L'avrete notato anche voi: non c'è una politica di sinistra, sia essa consigliera comunale di Vizzolo Predabissi o capa di partito, senatrice o ex ministra, che quando si parla della Meloni non ritenga urgente rimarcare che quella pericolosa retrograda si fa mettere l'articolo maschile. E, se è il rimprovero che è più sensato ripetere, capite bene che, oltre al mistero del perché alcuni vogliano candidarsi a sindaco, non è chiaro perché chiunque debba un domani votare la sinistra, quando al governo c'è evidentemente una donna perfetta, così perfetta che tutto ciò che hai da rimproverarle è l'articolo determinativo. You'll have noticed it too: there isn't a single left-wing politician, whether a town councillor in Vizzolo Predabissi or a party leader, a senator or a former minister, who, whenever Meloni comes up, doesn't feel the urgent need to point out that the dangerous reactionary has herself called by the masculine article. And if that's the criticism most worth repeating, you can see that, besides the mystery of why anyone would want to run for mayor, it isn't clear why anyone should ever vote for the left, when the government is evidently led by a perfect woman, so perfect that all you can reproach her for is the definite article. Related in anthology «Nello spirito ormai caratteristico della sinistra, quello di negare l'evidenza con l'orwelliana convinzione che se dici con una certa sicumera che le donne possono avere il cazzo poi l'elettorato non crederà ai propri occhi ma alle tue parole, i laburisti hanno ben pensato di dire per anni che l'aria condizionata non serve: basta aprire le finestre e fare un po' di corrente. E bere tanta acqua e non uscire nelle ore più calde.» — Same Soncini, same diagnosis: the left prefers its own words to the evidence of the facts."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/thoreau-palude-sacra.html", "t": "When I would recreate myself, I seek the darkest wood, the thickest and most interminable and, to the citizen, most dismal, swamp. I enter a swamp as a sacred place, a *sanctum sanctorum*.", "b": "Henry David Thoreau", "x": "When I would recreate myself, I seek the darkest wood, the thickest and most interminable and, to the citizen, most dismal, swamp. I enter a swamp as a sacred place, a sanctum sanctorum . via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Una noia necessaria Info & reflection: the most uncomfortable, darkest place as a sacred place. Renewal doesn't go through beauty. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Brondi quotes Thoreau entering the swamp as a sanctum sanctorum: renewal sought in the darkest place, the primeval forest under the city"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/umberto-bianco-guarire.html", "t": "La romanticizzazione della salute mentale su internet ci sta vendendo l'idea che guarire sia bere caffè in belle tazze e scrivere su un diario, quando in realtà \"guarire\" è un processo disgustoso, violento, solitario in cui sei tu che combatti con la tua mente.", "b": "Umberto Bianco", "x": "La romanticizzazione della salute mentale su internet ci sta vendendo l'idea che guarire sia bere caffè in belle tazze e scrivere su un diario, quando in realtà \"guarire\" è un processo disgustoso, violento, solitario in cui sei tu che combatti con la tua mente. The romanticisation of mental health on the internet is selling us the idea that healing means drinking coffee from pretty mugs and writing in a journal, when in reality \"healing\" is a disgusting, violent, lonely process in which it's you fighting with your own mind. Info & reflection: a clear criticism of how social media turns psychological healing into an aesthetic. The theme comes back in authors like Mira Rowen (\"Self-healing is not a pretty process. It's not clean. It's not cute. And it's not calm.\", from the book Self-Healing Isn't Pretty ) and in various pieces online in the \"healing is not an aesthetic\" vein (Glass Magazine and others). In the vault the same theme, suffering and healing performed for social consumption, is already there from another angle in ."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/varrone-filosofi-razionalizzano-tutto.html", "t": "No sick man's monstrous dream can be so wild that some philosopher won't say it's true.", "b": "Marcus Terentius Varro", "x": "\"No sick man's monstrous dream can be so wild that some philosopher won't say it's true.\" Info & reflection: A critique of philosophy from the inside, not the outside. It doesn't say philosophy is useless; it says it is so flexible it can justify any claim, however absurd. A healthy reminder against the pull of a well-built argument for its own sake: the logical coherence of a claim isn't enough to make it true, only to make it defensible. Related in anthology The more we justify our beliefs, the more narrow-minded we become. — Lin Yutang: narrow-mindedness comes before the rationalising that dresses it up as logic."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/venditti-dalla-amici-mai.html", "t": "1983. Antonello Venditti sta attraversando uno dei momenti più bui della sua vita: la sua compagna, l'attrice Simona Izzo, lo lascia per Maurizio Costanzo, il giornalista più potente della televisione italiana. Venditti crolla e scrive una canzone. Nel testo nasconde una frecciata precisa: inserisce \"e tutto il resto è vita\", la frase con cui Costanzo chiudeva ogni puntata del suo show — un colpo basso travestito da ballata. Nel mezzo del dolore cita Dante, l'Inferno, il canto quinto, l'amore che fa soffrire, l'amore che alla fine perdona. / Ma la storia più bella non è nel testo. L'amico del titolo non è una metafora: è Lucio Dalla, che si accorge che Venditti sta crollando e non lo lascia solo. Lo porta via da Roma, lo ospita per due anni nel suo castello — uno studio leggendario — dal cui rifugio nasce la rinascita. / La canzone nasce dal tradimento di una donna, sopravvive grazie all'amicizia di un uomo. L'amore ti porta all'inferno, l'amicizia ti riporta a casa.", "b": "Antonello Venditti", "x": "Antonello Venditti sta attraversando uno dei momenti più bui della sua vita: la sua compagna, l'attrice Simona Izzo, lo lascia per Maurizio Costanzo, il giornalista più potente della televisione italiana. Venditti crolla e scrive una canzone. Nel testo nasconde una frecciata precisa: inserisce \"e tutto il resto è vita\", la frase con cui Costanzo chiudeva ogni puntata del suo show — un colpo basso travestito da ballata. Nel mezzo del dolore cita Dante, l'Inferno, il canto quinto, l'amore che fa soffrire, l'amore che alla fine perdona. Ma la storia più bella non è nel testo. L'amico del titolo non è una metafora: è Lucio Dalla, che si accorge che Venditti sta crollando e non lo lascia solo. Lo porta via da Roma, lo ospita per due anni nel suo castello — uno studio leggendario — dal cui rifugio nasce la rinascita. La canzone nasce dal tradimento di una donna, sopravvive grazie all'amicizia di un uomo. L'amore ti porta all'inferno, l'amicizia ti riporta a casa. 1983. Antonello Venditti is going through one of the darkest moments of his life: his partner, the actress Simona Izzo, leaves him for Maurizio Costanzo, the most powerful journalist on Italian television. Venditti falls apart and writes a song. In the lyrics he hides a precise jab: he puts in \"e tutto il resto è vita\" (\"and all the rest is life\"), the line Costanzo closed every episode of his show with, a low blow dressed up as a ballad. In the middle of the pain he quotes Dante, the Inferno, the fifth canto, love that makes you suffer, love that in the end forgives. But the best story isn't in the lyrics. The friend in the title isn't a metaphor: it's Lucio Dalla, who sees Venditti falling apart and doesn't leave him alone. He takes him away from Rome and puts him up for two years in his castle, a legendary studio, and from that refuge comes the rebirth. The song is born from a woman's betrayal and survives thanks to a man's friendship. Love takes you to hell, friendship brings you back home. Songs mentioned Amici mai — Antonello Venditti · Spotify"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/viola-altruismo-circostanze.html", "t": "Ognuno vorrebbe scavare la brace con le mani degli altri (proverbio napoletano)", "b": "Ester Viola", "x": "Ognuno vorrebbe scavare la brace con le mani degli altri (proverbio napoletano) Everyone would like to dig in the embers with someone else's hands (Neapolitan proverb) Info & reflection: There's an unwritten but structural split in every human system: those who put their hands in the fire and solve problems for everyone, and those who stand and watch. It isn't about missed opportunities or bad circumstances. Some people simply weren't born to be better."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/vizzardelli-sequestro-ripetizione.html", "t": "Si attua un sequestro di persona prodotto dalla ripetizione. Si entra in una trance in cui – senza sforzo – si insiste.", "b": "Silvia Vizzardelli", "x": "Si attua un sequestro di persona prodotto dalla ripetizione. Si entra in una trance in cui – senza sforzo – si insiste. A kidnapping takes place, produced by repetition. You enter a trance in which, without effort, you keep going. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025) — Insistere Info & reflection: repetition as a technique of abduction: you get kidnapped by the practice, you don't choose to keep going. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — In the chapter Insistere Brondi quotes Vizzardelli: repetition that holds you captive in an effortless trance, where discipline and doing nothing are the same thing"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/von-preuschen-mors-imperator.html", "t": "Mors Imperator", "b": "Hermione von Preuschen", "x": "Info & reflection: Really interesting story, read the whole caption In 1887 the Berlin Academy rejected the painting: the skeleton dressed as an emperor was read as a mockery of the 90-year-old Wilhelm I. Von Preuschen showed it herself and the scandal made her famous. It is privately owned; from 22 March to 15 November 2026 it is on show at the Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin. Source: Wikipedia (de) , ArtRabbit ."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/walter-siti-soncini-letteratura-studio.html", "t": "Ascoltavo Walter Siti dire che bisognerebbe, a scuola, prima studiare la letteratura contemporanea, scritta in una lingua di più facile comprensione, e solo dopo arrivare a Dante, la cui grandezza a quel punto capisci più facilmente, e pensavo alla me quindicenne convinta che Dante avesse copiato Venditti, tale e quale alle trentenni di oggi che hanno riempito i social di «tutto qua?» dopo una certa scena del “Diavolo veste Prada 2”.", "b": "Guia Soncini, Walter Siti", "x": "Ascoltavo Walter Siti dire che bisognerebbe, a scuola, prima studiare la letteratura contemporanea, scritta in una lingua di più facile comprensione, e solo dopo arrivare a Dante, la cui grandezza a quel punto capisci più facilmente, e pensavo alla me quindicenne convinta che Dante avesse copiato Venditti, tale e quale alle trentenni di oggi che hanno riempito i social di «tutto qua?» dopo una certa scena del “Diavolo veste Prada 2”. I was listening to Walter Siti say that at school we should first study contemporary literature, written in a language that's easier to understand, and only afterwards get to Dante, whose greatness you then grasp more easily, and I thought of my fifteen-year-old self convinced that Dante had copied Venditti, just like today's thirty-year-olds who filled social media with \"that's it?\" after a certain scene in \"The Devil Wears Prada 2\"."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/washington-irving-cuore-gentile.html", "t": "A kind heart is a fountain of gladness, making everything in its vicinity freshen into smiles.", "b": "Washington Irving", "x": "\"A kind heart is a fountain of gladness, making everything in its vicinity freshen into smiles.\" Info & reflection: kindness as an effect on the surroundings, not only a personal virtue. It refreshes the people around it before anyone even judges it."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/watts-chatter-servo-padrone.html", "t": "A person who thinks all the time has nothing to think about except thoughts. So, he loses touch with reality, and lives in a world of illusions. By thoughts, I mean specifically, chatter in the skull. Perpetual and compulsive repetition of words, of reckoning and calculating. / Like everything else, it's useful in moderation. **A good servant but a bad master.** / And all so-called civilized peoples have increasingly become crazy and self-destructive because, through excessive thinking, they have lost touch with reality. That's to say, we confuse signs, words, numbers, symbols, and ideas with the real world.", "b": "Alan Watts", "x": "A person who thinks all the time has nothing to think about except thoughts. So, he loses touch with reality, and lives in a world of illusions. By thoughts, I mean specifically, chatter in the skull. Perpetual and compulsive repetition of words, of reckoning and calculating. Like everything else, it's useful in moderation. A good servant but a bad master. And all so-called civilized peoples have increasingly become crazy and self-destructive because, through excessive thinking, they have lost touch with reality. That's to say, we confuse signs, words, numbers, symbols, and ideas with the real world. Info & reflection: \"Chatter in the skull\" as the exact name for that voice that never stops. It isn't thought, it's noise that takes the place of reality. And the final diagnosis is radical: modern civilization has become self-destructive for exactly this reason. More abstraction, less contact with what's real. Related in anthology Most of us would have rather money than tangible wealth. And a great occasion is somehow spoiled for us unless photographed. And to read about it the next day in the newspaper is oddly more fun for us than the original event. — Same Watts passage: here the diagnosis of compulsive thinking, there the examples of money, photographs and the newspaper preferred to the real thing"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/watts-soldi-ricchezza-fotografia.html", "t": "Most of us would have rather money than tangible wealth. And a great occasion is somehow spoiled for us unless photographed. And to read about it the next day in the newspaper is oddly more fun for us than the original event.", "b": "Alan Watts", "x": "Most of us would have rather money than tangible wealth. And a great occasion is somehow spoiled for us unless photographed. And to read about it the next day in the newspaper is oddly more fun for us than the original event. Info & reflection: Three concrete, sharp examples of confusing the map with the territory: we prefer the symbol to the thing (money vs real wealth), the picture to the experience (photo vs moment), the news to the event. Photography saw this problem coming, and social media has made it even sharper. Related in anthology A person who thinks all the time has nothing to think about except thoughts. So, he loses touch with reality, and lives in a world of illusions. By thoughts, I mean specifically, chatter in the skull. Perpetual and compulsive repetition of words, of reckoning and calculating. / Like everything else, it's useful in moderation. A good servant but a bad master. / And all so-called civilized peoples have increasingly become crazy and self-destructive because, through excessive thinking, they have lost touch with reality. That's to say, we confuse signs, words, numbers, symbols, and ideas with the real world. — The twin quote from the same post: the chatter in the skull that cuts us off from reality, and these examples are its symptoms"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/weil-attenzione-generosita.html", "t": "Attention is the rarest and purest form of generosity.", "b": "Simone Weil", "x": "\"Attention is the rarest and purest form of generosity.\" Info & reflection: It turns the idea of prayer upside down: it's not in the words but in the quality of the gaze. Paying full attention (to a person, a text, a moment) is already a sacred act. In a world built on distraction, it's also a revolutionary act."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/weil-parola-silenzio.html", "t": "Human speech is a profanation when it is not preceded by a long silence.", "b": "Simone Weil", "x": "Human speech is a profanation when it is not preceded by a long silence. via: Vasco Brondi, Una cosa spirituale (Einaudi, 2025), chapter Urlare senza suono Info & reflection: silence is not the absence of speech but what makes it possible. Related in anthology Una cosa spirituale. Non fare niente, altre forme d'arte — Weil in the Urlare senza suono chapter, where Brondi defends his long silences between one record and the next"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/wild-grounded-find-me-unseen.html", "t": "Find me where the crowd is less, / the sky is blue / and the wind is free. / Find me in the midst of the forgotten, / the abandoned, / to the one who seeks my presence. / Find me in the company of the unseen.", "b": "Wild and Grounded Living", "x": "Find me where the crowd is less, the sky is blue and the wind is free. Find me in the midst of the forgotten, the abandoned, to the one who seeks my presence. Find me in the company of the unseen. Info & reflection: the anaphoric rhythm (\"Find me where... Find me in... Find me in...\") turns a physical landscape (a mountain river in Montana) into a lay prayer. The movement is towards the margins: the thinner crowd, the forgotten, the abandoned, the unseen, as if real presence were always found at the edges, not in the centre."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/woolf-pace-evitare-vita.html", "t": "You cannot find peace by avoiding life.", "b": "Virginia Woolf", "x": "You cannot find peace by avoiding life. Note on the attribution: It isn't by Virginia Woolf as a writer: it's a line spoken by the Woolf character in the film The Hours (2002, screenplay by David Hare from Michael Cunningham's novel)."}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/woolf-sguardo-altrui-prigione.html", "t": "The eyes of others our prisons; their thoughts our cages.", "b": "Virginia Woolf", "x": "The eyes of others our prisons; their thoughts our cages. Note on attribution: From An Unwritten Novel (1920). Related in anthology La conformità è una categoria sociale: serve agli altri per sapere cosa aspettarsi da noi, per classificarci, per collocarci in modo prevedibile all'interno di un ordine che non abbiamo scelto. L'autenticità è una categoria interiore: serve a noi per rimanere in contatto con ciò che sentiamo vero in ogni fase della nostra vita. Le due non sempre coincidono. Anzi, nei momenti di vera trasformazione quasi mai coincidono. — Social conformity against authenticity. La libertà non è la capacità di cambiare le circostanze esterne. È la capacità di rimanere fedeli alla propria natura profonda dentro qualsiasi circostanza. — freedom as faithfulness to your own nature, not to other people's gaze Being hated by idiots is the price you pay for not being one of them. — accepting other people's judgment as the price of integrity"}, {"k": "commonplace", "h": "commonplace/zola-we-are-like-books.html", "t": "We are like books. Most people only see our cover, the minority read only the introduction, many people believe the critics, and only few will know our content.", "b": "Émile Zola", "x": "We are like books. Most people only see our cover, the minority read only the introduction, many people believe the critics, and only few will know our content. Attribution: uncertain. It circulates as Émile Zola's, but it can't be found in his works. Related in anthology The man who has not the habit of reading is imprisoned in his immediate world... The moment he takes up a book, he immediately enters a different world. — Zola: we are like books that few really read, an echo of reading as entering other worlds."}];